OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026", "title": "Constant-factor hardness of uniform sparsest cut", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026.pdf", "family": "117", "family_title": "Uniform sparsest cut: hardness and semidefinite gaps", "abstract_html": "We prove that, for every fixed <i>C</i> &amp;gt; 1, approximating Uniform Sparsest Cut within factor <i>C</i> is NP-hard. The output graphs have nonnegative rational capacities and unit demand between every pair of distinct vertices.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1. Constant-factor hardness of Uniform Sparsest Cut", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For a finite undirected graph \\(G\\) on \\([N]\\), let \\(c_{ij}=c_{ji}\\geq 0\\) be rational capacities, with \\(c_{ii}=0\\), and define …", "statement": "<p>For a finite undirected graph \\(G\\) on \\([N]\\), let \\(c_{ij}=c_{ji}\\geq 0\\) be rational capacities, with \\(c_{ii}=0\\), and define\n\\[\n\\Phi(G)=\n\\min_{\\varnothing\\neq S\\subsetneq[N]}\n\\frac{\\sum_{i\\in S,\\ j\\notin S}c_{ij}}{|S|(N-|S|)}.\n\\]\nEvery unordered pair of distinct vertices has demand exactly one. Capacities and thresholds are encoded in binary.</p><p>For every fixed real constant \\(C&gt;1\\), there is a polynomial-time reduction from \\(3\\mathrm{CNF}\\) satisfiability to such graphs \\(G\\), together with a positive rational threshold \\(a\\), satisfying\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{the formula is satisfiable}&amp;\\Longrightarrow \\Phi(G)\\leq a,\\\\\n\\text{the formula is unsatisfiable}&amp;\\Longrightarrow \\Phi(G)&gt;Ca.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe graph size and the binary encoding lengths of all output numbers are polynomial in the formula size; their constants and exponents may depend on \\(C\\).</p><p>Consequently, approximating Uniform Sparsest Cut within any fixed factor \\(C&gt;1\\) is NP-hard, without an unproved complexity hypothesis.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(C&gt;1\\), and choose an integer \\(C'&gt;C\\). Put\n\\[\nA_0=10^{12},\\qquad\\kappa=C'A_0,\\qquad T_0=100\\kappa.\n\\]</p><p>Propositions 1–2 construct the finite bit-test system from the input formula. Proposition 3 gives its primitive valuation obstruction. Proposition 4 supplies the rare-conjunction criterion, and Proposition 5 uses it to construct the analytic score gap. Proposition 6 supplies the finite polynomial structure needed for rational compilation. Proposition 7 constructs a polynomial-size graph with exactly unit all-pairs demands and rational threshold \\(a=A_0/N^2\\), satisfying\n\\[\n\\text{YES}:\\Phi(G)\\leq a,\n\\qquad\n\\text{NO}:\\Phi(G)&gt;C'a&gt;Ca.\n\\]</p><p>A factor-\\(C\\) approximation returns in the YES case a cut of ratio at most \\(Ca&lt;C'a\\), whereas every cut in the NO case has ratio greater than \\(C'a\\). Comparing its output ratio with the rational threshold \\(C'a\\) therefore decides \\(3\\mathrm{CNF}\\) satisfiability. Since this source problem is NP-complete, the claimed approximation hardness follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1. Quadratic evaluation pencil", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix \\(0<\\lambda _0<1\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(0&lt;\\lambda _0&lt;1\\). For every sufficiently large power of two \\(r\\), put \\(q=r^2\\).</p><p>There is a deterministic algorithm which, given \\(d\\geq1\\), constructs\n\\[\nu_1,\\ldots,u_m\\in\\mathbb F_q^d\n\\]\nsuch that:\n\\[\nm=O(d),\n\\]\nthe list contains every standard basis vector of \\(\\mathbb F_q^d\\), and every homogeneous quadratic polynomial \\(Q\\) that is nonzero at some point of the list satisfies\n\\[\n\\#\\{j:Q(u_j)=0\\}\\leq\\lambda _0m.\n\\]\nThe running time is \\(2^{O(d)}\\) bit operations, with constants allowed to depend on the fixed \\(r\\).</p><p>For the construction, write \\(F=\\mathbb F_q\\), let \\(k\\) be an algebraic closure of \\(F\\), put \\(D=r+1\\), and choose \\(r\\) so that\n\\[\n\\frac{2D}{r^2-r}\\leq\\lambda _0.\n\\]\nIn characteristic two define\n\\[\nS(Y)=Y^r+Y,\\qquad\nf(Y)=\\frac{Y^{r+1}}{Y^r+Y}\n=\\frac{Y^r}{Y^{r-1}+1}\n=\\frac1{S(1/Y)}.\n\\]\nStarting with transcendental \\(x_1\\), use the tower\n\\[\nS(x_{i+1})=f(x_i).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(l\\) least such that \\(N=r^{l-1}\\geq d\\). By Lemmas 1.2–1.3, choose \\(d\\) independent functions in \\(L\\); their \\(d\\times(q-r)N\\) evaluation matrix has rank \\(d\\). Select \\(d\\) independent columns and left-multiply by the inverse of their square matrix. The resulting functions remain in \\(L\\), and their evaluation columns include all standard basis vectors.</p><p>Call the columns \\(u_1,\\ldots,u_m\\), where \\(m=(q-r)N\\). If a homogeneous quadratic \\(Q\\) is nonzero on this list, applying \\(Q\\) to the \\(d\\) functions gives a nonzero element of the product span. Lemma 1.3 gives\n\\[\n\\#\\{j:Q(u_j)=0\\}\\leq2DN\\leq\\lambda _0(q-r)N=\\lambda _0m.\n\\]\nBecause \\(N&lt;rd\\), the list has length \\(O_r(d)\\), and the algorithm takes \\(2^{O_r(d)}\\) time.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.2 and the local splitting calculation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(b\\in k((z))\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(b\\in k((z))\\).</p><p>If \\(b-S(g)\\in k[[z]]\\) for a specified \\(g\\in k((z))\\), then \\(X^r+X=b\\) splits into \\(r\\) distinct roots in \\(k((z))\\), and its local integral lattice is \\(k[[z]]^r\\).</p><p>If \\(\\operatorname{ord}_z b=-1\\), then \\(X^r+X-b\\) is irreducible over \\(k((z))\\). Its extension is \\(k((w))\\), where \\(w=1/X\\), and \\(z\\) has \\(w\\)-order \\(r\\). Its integral lattice has basis\n\\[\n1,w,\\ldots,w^{r-1}\n\\quad\\text{over }k[[z]],\n\\]\nwhose discriminant has \\(z\\)-order \\(2(r-1)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>In the splitting case, put \\(b_0=b-S(g)\\). There are exactly \\(r\\) choices of \\(\\beta\\in k\\) with\n\\[\n\\beta^r+\\beta=b_0(0);\n\\]\nthe derivative is one, and the roots differ by elements of \\(\\mathbb F_r\\). For each choice, the positive-order solution \\(u\\) of\n\\[\nS(u)=b_0-S(\\beta)\n\\]\nis uniquely determined coefficientwise by\n\\[\nu_n=b_n+\\mathbf1_{\\{r\\mid n\\}}u_{n/r}^r,\\qquad n&gt;0.\n\\]\nThus the roots are \\(g+\\beta+u\\).</p><p>For a simple pole, \\(q_0(z)=1/b(z)\\) has order one and a compositional inverse \\(\\psi\\). In \\(k((w))\\), set\n\\[\nz=\\psi\\!\\left(\\frac{w^r}{1+w^{r-1}}\\right).\n\\]\nThis defines an injective homomorphism \\(k((z))\\to k((w))\\), with\n\\[\nz=cw^r+\\cdots,\\qquad c\\neq0,\n\\]\nand \\(X=1/w\\) satisfies \\(X^r+X=b(z)\\).</p><p>The monomials \\(w^jz^n\\), \\(0\\leq j&lt;r\\), \\(n\\geq0\\), have distinct leading orders \\(j+rn\\), exhausting the nonnegative integers. Successively subtracting the lowest remaining term gives\n\\[\nk[[w]]=\\bigoplus_{j=0}^{r-1}w^jk[[z]].\n\\]\nThe sum is direct because the leading orders of its nonzero summands lie in distinct residue classes modulo \\(r\\). Multiplication by a sufficiently large power of \\(z\\) gives the analogous assertion for Laurent series. Hence\n\\[\n[k((w)):k((z))]=r,\n\\]\nwhich proves irreducibility. Separability follows from \\(S'(X)=1\\).</p><p>The displayed free module is closed under multiplication, so its elements are integral by the characteristic polynomial of multiplication. Conversely, an element of negative \\(w\\)-order cannot satisfy a monic polynomial over \\(k[[z]]\\), because its highest power would be the unique term of smallest order. Thus \\(k[[w]]\\) is the integral closure.</p><p>The embeddings send \\(X\\) to \\(X+c\\), \\(c\\in\\mathbb F_r\\), and \\(w\\) to\n\\[\nw_c=\\frac1{X+c}.\n\\]\nFor \\(c\\neq c'\\),\n\\[\nw_c-w_{c'}=\\frac{c'-c}{(X+c)(X+c')}\n\\]\nhas \\(w\\)-order two, or extended \\(z\\)-order \\(2/r\\). The evaluation matrix of \\(1,w,\\ldots,w^{r-1}\\) is Vandermonde. Its determinant squared is the trace-pairing determinant, whose order is\n\\[\n2\\binom r2\\frac2r=2(r-1).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Tower branches, integral lattices, and global functions; original Sections 2.2–2.4.** In the setting of Proposition 1, let the tower stop at \\(x_l\\), and put …", "statement": "<p>**Tower branches, integral lattices, and global functions; original Sections 2.2–2.4.**</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 1, let the tower stop at \\(x_l\\), and put\n\\[\nN=r^{l-1},\\qquad\ne_a=x_2^{a_2}\\cdots x_l^{a_l},\\qquad 0\\leq a_i&lt;r.\n\\]\nThen the tower is a field of degree \\(N\\) over \\(k(x_1)\\), and the \\(e_a\\) form a basis. The same degree and basis assertions hold over \\(F(x_1)\\).</p><p>At each base place, let \\(\\Lambda\\subset k((t))^N\\) be the product of the branch integral lattices in this coefficient basis. If there are \\(B\\) branches and \\(C\\) is an integral basis matrix, then\n\\[\n\\delta:=\\operatorname{ord}_t\\det C=N-B,\\qquad 0\\leq\\delta\\leq N.\n\\]</p><p>There is an explicitly specified \\(F\\)-space \\(L\\) of dimension at least \\(N\\), containing \\(1\\), whose functions are integral at all finite places and become integral at infinity after multiplication by \\(t^D\\), where \\(t=1/x_1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At a pole of \\(Y\\), \\(f(Y)-Y\\) is integral. Starting at infinity with \\(t=1/x_1\\), each step is therefore a simple-pole extension from Lemma 1.1. Inductively, each defining polynomial is irreducible over the preceding completion and hence over the preceding field. This proves the degree and basis assertions over \\(k(x_1)\\), and consequently over \\(F(x_1)\\).</p><p>At a finite point \\(x_1=a\\), use \\(t=x_1-a\\). If \\(a\\in\\mathbb F_r\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), the first right side has a simple pole, and every later step is again a simple-pole extension. If \\(a\\notin\\mathbb F_r\\), the first extension splits integrally. A residue \\(\\beta\\notin\\mathbb F_r\\) has \\(f(\\beta)\\neq0\\), so no root of \\(S(Y)=f(\\beta)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathbb F_r\\); induction gives only unshifted integral splittings.</p><p>Above zero, while the residues remain zero,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ord}_t x_j=r^{j-1}.\n\\]\nIf the first nonzero residue occurs at \\(x_i\\), it is \\(\\alpha\\in\\mathbb F_r^*\\), and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ord}_t(x_i-\\alpha)\n=\\operatorname{ord}_tS(x_i)=r^{i-1}.\n\\]\nModulo integral series,\n\\[\nf(x_i)\\equiv\\frac{\\alpha^2}{S(x_i)}\n=S\\!\\left(\\frac{\\alpha^2}{x_{i-1}}\\right).\n\\]\nFor an earlier small variable \\(x_j\\), \\(j&gt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\frac1{x_j}\\equiv\\frac1{S(x_j)}\n=S\\!\\left(\\frac1{x_{j-1}}\\right)\\pmod{k[[t]]},\n\\]\nbecause the difference of the first two terms is\n\\[\n\\frac{x_j^{r-2}}{1+x_j^{r-1}},\n\\]\nwhich is integral. Thus successive splitting steps have\n\\[\nx_{i+s}\\equiv\\frac{\\alpha^2}{x_{i-s}}\\pmod{k[[t]]},\n\\qquad 1\\leq s\\leq i-1,\n\\]\nfor as long as these variables occur. The last principal part is \\(\\alpha^2/x_1\\), a simple pole; thereafter all steps are simple-pole extensions. At each stage the described split factors or ramified factor account for the full relative degree \\(r\\), so no local factor is omitted.</p><p>For a matrix over \\(k[[t]]\\) with nonzero determinant, elementary invertible row and column operations give a diagonal matrix with diagonal entries units times powers of \\(t\\). Its cokernel dimension is the determinant order. For a branch \\(k((z))/k((t))\\), multiplication by \\(z\\) on \\(k[[z]]\\) has cokernel \\(k\\); multiplication by a unit has unit determinant. Hence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ord}_t\\operatorname{Norm}_{k((z))/k((t))}(z^au)=a,\n\\qquad a\\in\\mathbb Z,\\quad u\\in k[[z]]^*.\n\\]</p><p>For composed separable bases,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{disc}(b_ic_j)\n=\\operatorname{disc}(b_i)^r\n\\operatorname{Norm}\\bigl(\\operatorname{disc}(c_j)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThis follows by factoring the evaluation matrix, taking determinants, and squaring. The power basis has constant nonzero discriminant, because the roots at each additive step differ by elements of \\(\\mathbb F_r^*\\).</p><p>If a local algebra currently has degree \\(n\\), \\(B\\) branches, and integral discriminant order \\(d_{\\mathrm{int}}\\), and \\(R_0\\) branches ramify at the next step, then\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nd_{\\mathrm{int,new}}&amp;=r\\,d_{\\mathrm{int}}+2(r-1)R_0,\\\\\nB_{\\mathrm{new}}&amp;=rB-(r-1)R_0,\\\\\nn_{\\mathrm{new}}&amp;=rn.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nStarting with \\(d_{\\mathrm{int}}=0\\) and \\(n=B=1\\), these give\n\\[\nd_{\\mathrm{int}}=2(n-B).\n\\]\nChanging basis changes the trace matrix \\(G\\) to \\(C^TGC\\); since \\(\\det G\\) is a unit,\n\\[\n\\delta=N-B.\n\\]\nAt a good finite point \\(a\\notin\\mathbb F_r\\), both the power-basis lattice and the integral lattice have determinant order zero, so they coincide.</p><p>For finite precision, every nonzero \\(x_i-c\\), \\(c\\in\\mathbb F_r\\), has absolute order at most \\(N^2\\) in every partial branch. Before subsequent ramification the zero-branch and descending-pole orders are at most \\(N\\), and later ramification multiplies them by at most \\(N\\). Set\n\\[\nF_0=(l+1)rN^2,\\qquad H=(2N-1)F_0+D.\n\\]\nThen \\(t^{F_0}e_a\\) is integral in every branch. Trace bounds and Cramer's rule give\n\\[\nt^{F_0}k[[t]]^N\\subseteq\\Lambda_a\\subseteq t^{-H}k[[t]]^N\n\\quad(a\\in\\mathbb F_r),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nt^{F_0-D}k[[t]]^N\n\\subseteq t^{-D}\\Lambda_\\infty\n\\subseteq t^{-H}k[[t]]^N.\n\\]\nIndeed, entries of \\(G\\) have order at least \\(-2F_0\\); the traces of \\(ye_a\\) have order at least \\(-F_0-D\\) whenever \\(t^Dy\\) is integral; and cofactors of \\(G\\) have order at least \\(-2F_0(N-1)\\). Membership in these lattices is determined by coefficient jets from exponent \\(-H\\) through \\(F_0-1\\).</p><p>Let \\(V_H\\) consist of coefficient vectors whose entries lie in the span of\n\\[\n1,x_1,\\ldots,x_1^H,\\qquad\n(x_1-a)^{-j}\\quad(a\\in\\mathbb F_r,\\ 1\\leq j\\leq H).\n\\]\nThese rational functions are independent by their principal parts and polynomial parts, so\n\\[\n\\dim_kV_H=N[1+(r+1)H].\n\\]\nImpose integrality at the \\(r\\) bad finite places and membership in \\(t^{-D}\\Lambda_\\infty\\) at infinity. The quotient dimensions are \\(NH+\\delta_a\\) at finite places and \\(NH+\\delta_\\infty-ND\\) at infinity. The resulting space \\(L_k\\) therefore satisfies\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dim_kL_k\n&amp;\\geq N[1+(r+1)H]\n-\\left((r+1)NH+\\sum_{a\\in\\mathbb F_r\\cup\\{\\infty\\}}\\delta_a-ND\\right)\\\\\n&amp;\\geq N(D-r)=N.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nNo conditions are needed elsewhere. The constant \\(1\\) belongs to this space.</p><p>Coefficientwise \\(q\\)-Frobenius preserves the tower equations and permutes the exceptional branches, hence preserves \\(L_k\\). Its reduced row echelon basis is unique, so Frobenius fixes every entry of that basis. Thus \\(L_k\\) is defined over \\(F\\), and its \\(F\\)-rational space \\(L\\) has\n\\[\n\\dim_FL=\\dim_kL_k\\geq N.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Evaluation bound and effective construction; original Sections 2.5–2.6.** For the space \\(L\\) of Lemma 1.2, there are \\((q-r)N\\) rational evaluation tuples above \\(x_1\\in F\\setminus\\mathbb F_r\\).", "statement": "<p>**Evaluation bound and effective construction; original Sections 2.5–2.6.**</p><p>For the space \\(L\\) of Lemma 1.2, there are \\((q-r)N\\) rational evaluation tuples above \\(x_1\\in F\\setminus\\mathbb F_r\\).</p><p>Every nonzero function in the span of products of two elements of \\(L_k\\) vanishes at at most \\(2DN\\) of these positions. Evaluation is injective on \\(L\\).</p><p>The space \\(L\\), its evaluations, and independent evaluation columns can be computed in \\(2^{O_r(N)}\\) bit operations.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(\\beta\\in F\\setminus\\mathbb F_r\\), then\n\\[\n\\beta^r+\\beta\\in\\mathbb F_r^*,\\qquad\n\\beta^{r+1}\\in\\mathbb F_r^*,\n\\]\nso \\(f(\\beta)\\in\\mathbb F_r^*\\). The map \\(S:F\\to\\mathbb F_r\\) is \\(\\mathbb F_r\\)-linear with kernel \\(\\mathbb F_r\\), and is onto. Each next equation has exactly \\(r\\) roots in \\(F\\setminus\\mathbb F_r\\). Induction gives \\((q-r)N\\) tuples.</p><p>Let \\(a\\neq0\\) lie in the product span. It is integral at every finite place, and \\(t^{2D}a\\) is integral at infinity. Its nonzero field norm to \\(k(x_1)\\) has no finite poles and is a polynomial. Since\n\\[\n0\\leq\\operatorname{ord}_t\\operatorname{Norm}(t^{2D}a)\n=2DN+\\operatorname{ord}_t\\operatorname{Norm}(a),\n\\]\nits degree is at most \\(2DN\\). At a good base value \\(b\\), the local norm is the product of the \\(N\\) integral branch values, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ord}_{x_1-b}\\operatorname{Norm}(a)\n=\\sum_{j=1}^N\\operatorname{ord}_{x_1-b}(a_j).\n\\]\nEach zero evaluation contributes at least one, including multiple zero branches above one base value. Hence the total is at most \\(2DN\\). Since\n\\[\n2DN&lt;(q-r)N,\n\\]\nevaluation is injective on \\(L\\).</p><p>For effectivity, construct the fixed finite fields once. At a splitting step over a finite constant field \\(F_Q\\supseteq F\\), any solution of \\(S(\\beta)\\in F_Q\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\beta^Q-\\beta\\in\\mathbb F_r,\\qquad \\beta^{Q^2}=\\beta.\n\\]\nThus one quadratic extension per level suffices. Such an extension is constructed by binary linear algebra: \\(y\\mapsto y^2+y\\) has image of codimension one, so a vector outside its image gives an irreducible polynomial \\(Y^2+Y+b\\). Solve \\(S(\\beta)=b_0\\) by binary linear algebra in the extension. All constants may be put in a common field of degree\n\\[\nE=2^{l-1}\\leq N\n\\]\nover \\(F\\).</p><p>Put \\(M_0=N^2\\). A nonzero elementary factor \\(x_i-c\\) of order between \\(-M_0\\) and \\(M_0\\), known modulo parameter power \\(K&gt;M_0\\), has relative error order at least \\(K-M_0\\). Multiplication, integer powers, and inversion preserve this relative accuracy. Products and quotients of a fixed number of these factors have absolute accuracy at least \\(K-C_rM_0\\). These rules cover \\(f\\), the prescribed shifts, and their images under \\(S\\); factor \\(Y^{r-1}+1\\) over \\(\\mathbb F_r^*\\) if needed.</p><p>Subtraction preserves absolute accuracy even when principal parts cancel. For splitting, the branch calculation already proves that the shifted right side is integral, and the coefficient recursion determines the required positive coefficients. For a simple-pole step, compositional reversion uses only coefficients through the requested order and divides by a nonzero linear coefficient. If the new \\(z(w)\\) is known modulo \\(w^B\\), substitution into an old series of pole order at most \\(M_0\\) is accurate through\n\\[\nB-r-rM_0.\n\\]\nThus \\(B\\geq L+r(M_0+1)\\) suffices for output precision \\(L\\); old coefficients through \\(\\lceil L/r\\rceil+M_0+2\\) suffice.</p><p>Choose a fixed \\(a_r\\) so that \\(K_N=(2N)^{a_r}\\) dominates all fixed losses. A uniform backward budget is\n\\[\nL_{i-1}=K_N(L_i+K_N+H+D).\n\\]\nFor a rational coefficient basis input times a power-basis monomial, the final pole order is bounded by\n\\[\nP_0=HN+(r-1)(l-1)N^2+DN.\n\\]\nA sufficiently large fixed multiple\n\\[\nL_{\\mathrm{end}}=C_r'(H+D+l+1)N^2\n\\]\ntherefore determines every negative coefficient, including after the infinity multiplier. Unknown tails of individual inputs are already integral, so cancellation in a linear combination cannot reveal an uncomputed negative coefficient.</p><p>Run the budget backward and the branch expansions forward. For each basis input, record all negative coefficients in all exceptional branches, including the infinity adjustment. Their vanishing is exactly the linear system defining \\(L\\).</p><p>There are at most \\((r+1)N\\) exceptional branches. Moreover,\n\\[\nF_0=O_r(N^3),\\quad H=O_r(N^4),\n\\]\nthere are \\(O_r(N^5)\\) coefficient unknowns, and \\(L_{\\mathrm{end}}=O_r(N^6)\\). There are \\(O_r(N^7)\\) negative-coefficient equations over the constant fields, or \\(O_r(N^8)\\) equations over \\(F\\). The retained series lengths are at most\n\\[\n(2N)^{O_r(l+1)}.\n\\]\nElementary truncated arithmetic, composition, and coefficientwise reversion use polynomially many field operations in these lengths. As \\(l=1+\\log_rN\\), their total bit complexity is \\(2^{O_r(N)}\\). Evaluation and column selection add only polynomial time in the matrix sizes.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Theorem 3.1. A bit-test system with mixing parity queries", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(\\lambda>0\\), there are constants \\(\\eta>0\\), \\(B<\\infty\\), and a fixed finite list of bounded-arity test laws with the following properties.", "statement": "<p>For every fixed \\(\\lambda&gt;0\\), there are constants \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), \\(B&lt;\\infty\\), and a fixed finite list of bounded-arity test laws with the following properties.</p><p>From a Boolean \\(3\\mathrm{CNF}\\) formula, one can construct in polynomial time a finite uniform space \\(\\mathcal S\\) of bit positions and these test laws such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every individual question marginal is at most \\(B\\) times uniform measure on \\(\\mathcal S\\). Samplers are polynomially enumerable finite rational weighted lists, with inverse-polynomial positive seed probabilities.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. A satisfiable formula has an assignment \\(y:\\mathcal S\\to\\mathbb F_2\\) accepted by every seeded test.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For an unsatisfiable formula, every assignment fails some test law with probability greater than \\(\\eta\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. There is an exchangeable law of \\((s_1,s_2,s_3,s_4)\\in\\mathcal S^4\\), with uniform marginals, whose conditional expectation operator for every pair of distinct coordinates has mean-zero \\(L^2\\)-norm at most \\(\\lambda\\). The perfect assignment satisfies\n\\[\ny_{s_1}+y_{s_2}+y_{s_3}+y_{s_4}=0\n\\]\nthroughout its support.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. Two independent copies, paired coordinatewise, have the same pair-operator bound on \\(\\mathcal S^2\\) and give the parity identity for \\(y_i+y_j\\).</p><p>Construction setting: pad variable addresses to \\(\\{0,1\\}^d\\), with \\(d=O(\\log(\\text{input size}+1))\\). Use Proposition 1 over a fixed square field \\(F=\\mathbb F_q\\), and put\n\\[\nG_0=\\operatorname{span}_F\\{u_ju_j^T:1\\leq j\\leq m\\},\n\\qquad\nG=\\operatorname{Mat}_{101\\times101}(G_0).\n\\]\nGive \\(G\\) uniform measure \\(\\mu\\). It contains every diagonal matrix in each block, and \\(\\dim_FG=O(d)\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal D\\) be the direction law\n\\[\nh=(c\\otimes u_j)(e\\otimes u_j)^T,\n\\]\nwhere \\(c,e\\) are independent uniform elements of \\(F^{101}\\), and \\(j\\) is uniform in \\([m]\\). Fix a nonzero \\(\\mathbb F_2\\)-linear functional \\(\\ell:F\\to\\mathbb F_2\\), and use\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=G\\times F^*,\\qquad y_{(A,\\sigma)}=\\ell(\\sigma P(A))\n\\]\nfor a field oracle \\(P:G\\to F\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Pad nonempty short clauses by repeated literals. Replace an empty clause by opposing unit clauses on a fresh variable and pad them. A formula with no clauses may be replaced by a tautological clause. These operations preserve satisfiability.</p><p>Choose the field and pencil so that\n\\[\n\\lambda _1&lt;10^{-6},\\qquad\n\\max\\{\\lambda _1,2/(q-3)\\}\\leq\\lambda,\\qquad\n6/(q-1)\\leq1/16.\n\\]\nThe full mask vector at \\(A\\) determines \\(P(A)\\) by nondegeneracy. Implement every field query by all masks, rejecting invalid encodings.</p><p>On Boolean diagonal \\(T=\\operatorname{diag}(z)\\), the principal minors \\(p_I\\) are the Boolean monomials. Every Boolean table has a unique expansion in them. For \\(a\\in\\{0,1\\}^3\\), let \\(E_a(T)\\) extend the table indicating that the addressed ordered triple supports a clause whose falsifying pattern is \\(a\\).</p><p>Use one assignment field \\(X\\), depending only on the first diagonal block. Let \\(\\pi_i\\) swap block one and block \\(i\\), and put \\(X_i(A)=X(\\pi_iA)\\). Define\n\\[\nM_{a,i}=\n\\begin{cases}\nX_i,&amp;a_i=1,\\\\\n1-X_i,&amp;a_i=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nIntroduce \\(Y_a,Z_a\\), with the \\(25\\) desired Boolean-diagonal identities\n\\[\nX^2-X=0,\\quad\nY_a-E_aM_{a,1}=0,\\quad\nZ_a-M_{a,2}M_{a,3}=0,\\quad\nY_aZ_a=0.\n\\]\nFor a satisfying assignment, extend the assignment table and the Boolean gate tables by principal minors. Each identity difference is a linear combination of products of two principal minors and vanishes on Boolean diagonals. Lemma 2.3 supplies three slack fields for each identity.</p><p>There are \\(1+16+75=92\\) fields. Give each a selector index in a fresh block, in addition to the three address and six tag blocks. For a term \\(cMN\\) of a field \\(F_1\\), append its selector row and column to the first minor. Summing the packed terms gives \\(P\\), with\n\\[\n\\Delta_{v_{F_1}}P=F_1,\n\\]\nwhere \\(v_{F_1}\\) is the selector unit diagonal. Other fields omit that selector and do not contribute.</p><p>Every minor of \\(A+th\\) is affine in \\(t\\): terms using two perturbed columns vanish because those columns are proportional. Thus every packed product, and \\(P\\), has line degree at most two.</p><p>For an arbitrary oracle, define the fields by selector differences. The ordinary laws are:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Check line degree at most two using every \\(t\\in F\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For each of the \\(25\\) identities, check its slack representation from Lemma 2.3 at uniform \\(A\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For uniform \\(v\\in G\\) whose first diagonal block is zero, check \\(X(A+v)=X(A)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. For every required slack bank \\(B_r\\), check\n\\[\n\\Delta_w\\Delta_vL(A)=0\n\\]\nfor independent uniform \\(v,w\\in B_r\\), and separately for each fixed scalar \\(a\\in F\\), check\n\\[\n\\Delta_{av}L(A)=a\\Delta_vL(A).\n\\]</p><p>All master queries are uniform on \\(G\\). Fixed translations and block swaps are bijections. The tag shears are bijections because they leave their source \\(T\\) fixed, and selector compositions preserve this property. A fixed mask has density at most \\(q-1\\) relative to uniform \\(\\mathcal S\\), so \\(B=q-1\\). The honest oracle passes identically, and Lemma 2.2 gives parity completeness.</p><p>For soundness, suppose every binary law fails with probability at most \\(\\eta\\). Decode valid mask vectors and choose arbitrary values elsewhere. Each decoded field law also fails with probability at most \\(\\eta\\). Let \\(Q\\) bound its master queries; \\(Q=q+32\\) suffices. Put \\(\\delta=\\sqrt\\eta\\). Lemma 2.4 corrects \\(P\\) to \\(P'\\). Every field-law failure increases by at most \\(2Q\\delta\\), since query marginals are uniform. Write\n\\[\n\\tau=\\eta+2Q\\delta.\n\\]\nIntersect the pullbacks of the good direction set under \\(\\pi_1,\\pi_2,\\pi_3\\). Its missing mass is at most \\(K_{\\mathrm{sw}}\\delta\\), where \\(K_{\\mathrm{sw}}=3\\). Fixed translates of corrected fields have degree at most two there. Lemma 2.5, using the conservative degree bound six, gives support gap\n\\[\n\\kappa=1-K_{\\mathrm{sw}}\\delta-\\frac6{q-1}-\\lambda _1.\n\\]</p><p>For a fixed tested assignment translation, a nonzero discrepancy has support at least \\(\\kappa\\). Thus translations preserving \\(X\\) identically have density at least \\(1-\\tau/\\kappa\\). They form a subgroup. Density greater than one half forces the subgroup to be the whole translation group, so assignment invariance is exact.</p><p>For a slack field and bank \\(B_r\\), define\n\\[\nW_v=\\{w\\in B_r:\\Delta_vL(A+w)=\\Delta_vL(A)\\text{ for every }A\\}.\n\\]\nEach \\(W_v\\) is a subgroup. The fraction of \\((v,w)\\) with a nonzero discrepancy is at most \\(\\tau/\\kappa\\). If \\(\\tau/\\kappa&lt;1/16\\), more than three quarters of \\(v\\) have more than three quarters valid \\(w\\), hence \\(W_v=B_r\\). The set of such \\(v\\) is a subgroup, by the difference cocycle, so it too is all of \\(B_r\\). Thus \\(v\\mapsto\\Delta_vL(A)\\) is additive. For each scalar \\(a\\), the map\n\\[\nv\\longmapsto\n\\bigl(\\Delta_{av}L(A)-a\\Delta_vL(A)\\bigr)_{A\\in G}\n\\]\nis additive and has kernel density greater than one half. Its kernel is all of \\(B_r\\). This proves exact \\(F\\)-affinity.</p><p>Choose bank bases \\(e_i,f_j\\). Exact separate affinity gives\n\\[\n\\Delta_{v(T)}\\Delta_{w(T)}L(A)\n=\\sum_{i,j}v_i(T)w_j(T)\\Delta_{e_i}\\Delta_{f_j}L(A).\n\\]\nThe coefficient functions have line degree at most two; \\(v_i,w_j\\) have degree at most one, and coordinates of \\(u(T)\\) have degree at most two. Therefore every slack discrepancy and every full identity discrepancy has degree at most four. Its failure fraction is at most \\(\\tau\\), so \\(\\tau&lt;\\kappa\\) forces it to vanish identically.</p><p>On Boolean diagonals, \\(v(T)=u(T)=0\\); the slack terms vanish and all \\(25\\) identities hold exactly. Invariance makes \\(X\\) a well-defined assignment on Boolean addresses. Booleanity gives bits, and if a clause were falsified, its gate identities would force \\(Y_a=Z_a=1\\), contradicting \\(Y_aZ_a=0\\).</p><p>For all smallness requirements, take\n\\[\nd_0=\\min\\left\\{\\frac\\gamma{16},\\frac1{16K_{\\mathrm{sw}}},\n\\frac1{128(1+2Q)}\\right\\},\n\\qquad 0&lt;\\eta\\leq d_0^2.\n\\]\nThen the correction assumptions hold, \\(\\kappa&gt;1/2\\), \\(\\tau\\leq1/128\\), \\(\\tau/\\kappa&lt;1/16\\), and \\(\\tau&lt;\\kappa\\). An unsatisfiable formula therefore has a law failing with probability greater than \\(\\eta\\).</p><p>Finally, \\(|G|=q^{O(d)}=2^{O(d)}\\). The samplers enumerate fixed products of polynomial-size spaces, fields, and the \\(O(d)\\)-length pencil. Public interpolation uses at most \\(2^{3d}\\) addresses. All arities and numbers of laws are fixed, and all positive seed probabilities are inverse-polynomial rationals. Proposition 1 supplies the pencil in polynomial time for \\(d=O(\\log(\\text{input size}+1))\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.2. Matrix-walk spectral bound", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the construction setting of Proposition 2, let \\( Tf(A)=\\mathbb E_{h\\sim\\mathcal D}f(A+h), \\qquad \\lambda _1=\\lambda _0+q^{-1}. \\) Then \\(T\\) has norm at most \\(\\lambda _1\\) on mean-zero \\(L^2(G)\\).", "statement": "<p>In the construction setting of Proposition 2, let\n\\[\nTf(A)=\\mathbb E_{h\\sim\\mathcal D}f(A+h),\n\\qquad \\lambda _1=\\lambda _0+q^{-1}.\n\\]\nThen \\(T\\) has norm at most \\(\\lambda _1\\) on mean-zero \\(L^2(G)\\). In particular, for every nonempty \\(D\\subseteq G\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{\\langle\\mathbf1_D,T\\mathbf1_D\\rangle}{\\mu(D)}\n\\leq\\mu(D)+\\lambda _1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Every additive character of \\(G\\) has the form\n\\[\nA\\longmapsto(-1)^{\\ell(L(A))}\n\\]\nfor an \\(F\\)-linear functional \\(L:G\\to F\\). The pairing \\((a,x)\\mapsto\\ell(ax)\\) is nondegenerate, and a basis and counting give all characters.</p><p>For nonzero \\(L\\),\n\\[\nL(h)=c^TM_je,\n\\]\nwhere the entries of \\(M_j\\) are homogeneous quadratics in \\(u_j\\). Some entry is nonzero somewhere on the list, since the directions span \\(G\\). By Proposition 1, that entry vanishes at at most \\(\\lambda _0m\\) indices. For \\(M_j\\neq0\\), averaging first over \\(c\\) gives zero unless \\(M_je=0\\), so the character average is\n\\[\nq^{-\\operatorname{rank}M_j}\\leq q^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe remaining indices contribute at most one. Characters diagonalize \\(T\\), proving the norm bound. Apply it to \\(\\mathbf1_D-\\mu(D)\\) for the final inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.3. Masked parity law", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 2, choose uniformly ordered distinct \\(t_1,t_2,t_3,t_4\\in F\\), independently uniform \\(A\\in G\\), \\(h\\sim\\mathcal D\\), and uniform \\(\\sigma\\in F^*\\).", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 2, choose uniformly ordered distinct \\(t_1,t_2,t_3,t_4\\in F\\), independently uniform \\(A\\in G\\), \\(h\\sim\\mathcal D\\), and uniform \\(\\sigma\\in F^*\\). Put\n\\[\n\\alpha_i=\\left(\\prod_{j\\neq i}(t_i-t_j)\\right)^{-1},\n\\qquad\ns_i=(A+t_ih,\\sigma\\alpha_i).\n\\]\nThis law is exchangeable with uniform marginals, and every pair operator has mean-zero norm at most\n\\[\n\\max\\{\\lambda _1,2/(q-3)\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(P\\) has degree at most two on every sampled line, then its bit encoding satisfies the four-bit parity identity throughout this law.</p>", "proof": "<p>Exchangeability and uniform marginals are immediate from the sampling. Lagrange interpolation gives\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\alpha_iP(A+t_ih)=0\n\\]\nfor a degree-two line restriction, and applying \\(\\ell(\\sigma\\,\\cdot)\\) gives parity.</p><p>For the pair bound, write the first times as \\(a,b\\) and the others as \\(c,d\\). Conditioning on the entire first bit position preserves the time and direction law, since its conditional distribution for each fixed time tuple and direction is uniform on \\(G\\times F^*\\). Given first position \\((x,m)\\), the second is\n\\[\n(x+(b-a)h,m\\alpha_b/\\alpha_a).\n\\]\nFor each time tuple, \\((b-a)h\\) has law \\(\\mathcal D\\), independently of the mask multiplier. Thus the pair operator is the tensor product of \\(T\\) and an averaged mask permutation.</p><p>For fixed distinct \\(a,b,c\\), put \\(k=(b-c)/(a-c)\\). In characteristic two,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\alpha_a}{\\alpha_b}=k\\,\\frac{b-d}{a-d}.\n\\]\nThis fractional-linear map sends excluded projective arguments \\(a,b,c,\\infty\\) to \\(\\infty,0,k^2,k\\). Hence for allowed \\(d\\) the ratio is uniform on\n\\[\nF^*\\setminus\\{k,k^2\\},\n\\]\nwith two distinct omitted values. The inverse ratio also omits two values.</p><p>On mean-zero mask functions, the sum of all \\(q-1\\) multiplicative permutations is zero; removing two leaves an average of norm at most \\(2/(q-3)\\). The spatially centered subspace has norm at most \\(\\lambda _1\\), while mask-only mean-zero functions have this latter bound. These orthogonal subspaces exhaust the nonconstant functions.</p><p>For two independent copies, decompose each factor into constants and mean-zero functions. The tensor-square pair operator has the same nonconstant bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.4. Polynomial slack", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(T\\) be the principal matrix on the first three address blocks, with \\(n=3d\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(T\\) be the principal matrix on the first three address blocks, with \\(n=3d\\). For \\(I\\subseteq[n]\\), let\n\\[\np_I(T)=\\det T_{I,I},\\qquad p_\\varnothing=1.\n\\]\nEvery linear combination of products \\(p_Ip_J\\) that vanishes on all Boolean diagonal matrices is a sum of terms\n\\[\ncMNT_{ij},\\qquad\ncMNT_{ij}(T_{ii}-1),\\qquad\ncMN(T_{ii}^2-T_{ii}),\n\\]\nwhere \\(i\\neq j\\) in the first two forms, \\(c\\in F\\), and \\(M,N\\) are arbitrary square minors, possibly empty.</p><p>Using two disjoint three-block tag banks, each such identity difference \\(F(A)\\) can be represented as\n\\[\nF(A)=\\Delta_{v(T)}L_1(A)\n+\\Delta_{v(T)}\\Delta_{w(T)}L_2(A)\n+\\Delta_{u(T)}L_3(A),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\Delta_VL(A)=L(A+V)-L(A)\\), \\(v(T)\\) copies the off-diagonal part of \\(T\\) into bank one, \\(w(T)\\) copies \\(\\operatorname{diag}(T)-I\\) into bank two, and \\(u(T)\\) copies \\(\\operatorname{diag}(T)^2-\\operatorname{diag}(T)\\) into bank one.</p><p>The fields \\(L_1,L_3\\) are affine under bank-one translations, and \\(L_2\\) is separately affine in each bank.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(i\\in K\\), row expansion gives\n\\[\np_K=T_{ii}p_{K\\setminus\\{i\\}}+\\sum_{j\\neq i}T_{ij}C_{ij}^K,\n\\]\nwhere each nonzero cofactor is a scalar sign times an arbitrary minor.</p><p>If \\(i\\in J\\setminus I\\), cancellation of diagonal terms gives\n\\[\np_Ip_J-p_{I\\cup\\{i\\}}p_{J\\setminus\\{i\\}}\n=\n\\sum_{j\\neq i}T_{ij}\n\\left(p_IC_{ij}^J-C_{ij}^{I\\cup\\{i\\}}p_{J\\setminus\\{i\\}}\\right).\n\\]\nIf \\(i\\in I\\cap J\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_Ip_J-p_Ip_{J\\setminus\\{i\\}}\n={}&amp;(T_{ii}^2-T_{ii})p_{I\\setminus\\{i\\}}p_{J\\setminus\\{i\\}}\\\\\n&amp;+\\sum_{j\\neq i}T_{ij}p_IC_{ij}^J\n+\\sum_{j\\neq i}T_{ij}(T_{ii}-1)C_{ij}^Ip_{J\\setminus\\{i\\}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nEach step removes an element of the second set and preserves the union. After \\(|J|\\) steps the canonical term is \\(p_{I\\cup J}\\). Telescoping reduces the given combination to \\(\\sum_Ub_Up_U\\) plus permitted residuals. On the indicator diagonal of \\(S\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{U\\subseteq S}b_U=0.\n\\]\nInduction on \\(|S|\\) gives \\(b_U=0\\).</p><p>For a minor \\(M(T)=\\det T_{R,C}\\), append a tag-bank row and column:\n\\[\nE_{ij}^{(r)}M(A)\n=\\det A_{(R,b_r(i)),(C,b_r(j))}.\n\\]\nFor \\(V\\) supported in principal bank \\(r\\), the only translated bank entry in this minor is its appended corner, whose cofactor is \\(M\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\Delta_V(E_{ij}^{(r)}M)\n=V_{b_r(i),b_r(j)}M.\n\\]\nPlace the residual terms into fields as follows:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\ncT_{ij}MN &amp; c(E_{ij}^{(1)}M)N\\text{ in }L_1\\\\\ncT_{ij}(T_{ii}-1)MN\n&amp;c(E_{ij}^{(1)}M)(E_{ii}^{(2)}N)\\text{ in }L_2\\\\\nc(T_{ii}^2-T_{ii})MN\n&amp;c(E_{ii}^{(1)}M)N\\text{ in }L_3.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe stated difference representation follows. The shifts belong to \\(G\\): copying blocks preserves \\(G_0\\), and every diagonal matrix belongs to \\(G_0\\). They leave \\(T\\) fixed.</p><p>Each extended factor is affine in its own bank and independent of the other bank under principal-bank translations. This proves the affinity assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Lemma 3.5. Deterministic correction of almost quadratic lines", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(P:G\\to F\\), and suppose the law sampling uniform \\(A\\) and \\(h\\sim\\mathcal D\\), and checking degree at most two on all \\(A+th\\), fails with probability at most \\(\\delta^2\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(P:G\\to F\\), and suppose the law sampling uniform \\(A\\) and \\(h\\sim\\mathcal D\\), and checking degree at most two on all \\(A+th\\), fails with probability at most \\(\\delta^2\\). Put \\(\\gamma=10^{-3}\\), and assume\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\delta+\\lambda _1&amp;&lt;\\gamma/2,\\\\\n1-4\\gamma-4\\delta-2/(q-1)&amp;&gt;\\delta+\\lambda _1,\\\\\n27\\gamma+5\\delta&amp;&lt;1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThen there is a scalar-invariant direction set \\(H\\) of \\(\\mathcal D\\)-mass at least \\(1-\\delta\\), and \\(P':G\\to F\\), differing from \\(P\\) on at most \\(2\\delta\\) of \\(G\\), such that every restriction\n\\[\nt\\longmapsto P'(A+th),\\qquad h\\in H,\n\\]\nhas degree at most two at every base \\(A\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(H\\) consist of directions with failing-base fraction at most \\(\\delta\\). Mark points whose failing-direction fraction exceeds \\(\\delta\\), obtaining \\(D_0\\) of density at most \\(\\delta\\). Markov's inequality gives \\(\\mathcal D(H)\\geq1-\\delta\\). Both conditions are scalar-invariant.</p><p>Enlarge \\(D_0\\) by adding an outside point \\(x\\) whenever\n\\[\n\\Pr_h[x+h\\in D]\\geq\\gamma.\n\\]\nIf \\(D_0\\) has \\(k\\geq1\\) points and the enlargement first reaches size \\(2k\\), its internal directed edge mass divided by density is at least \\(\\gamma/2\\). Lemma 2.1 bounds it by \\(2\\delta+\\lambda _1&lt;\\gamma/2\\), a contradiction. If \\(k=0\\), nothing can be added. Thus the final \\(D\\) has density at most \\(2\\delta\\). Outside points are clean and have entry probability less than \\(\\gamma\\) into \\(D\\).</p><p>Fix \\(h\\in H\\) and four distinct times \\(t_i\\). Let \\(J\\) be the bases whose four positions are clean but whose interpolation relation fails. Then \\(\\mu(J)\\leq\\delta\\). For \\(A\\in J\\), write \\(z_i=A+t_ih\\). Sample independent \\(k\\sim\\mathcal D\\) and uniform \\(a\\in F^*\\). Since \\(z_i\\notin D_0\\), all four transverse restrictions are quadratic except with probability at most \\(4\\delta\\). On that complement their discrepancy is a degree-two polynomial nonzero at zero, so vanishes at at most two nonzero scalars. Each \\(z_i+ak\\) is missing with probability at most \\(\\gamma\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\Pr_{k,a}[A+ak\\in J]\n\\geq1-4\\delta-4\\gamma-\\frac2{q-1}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(ak\\) has law \\(\\mathcal D\\), a nonempty \\(J\\) contradicts Lemma 2.1 and the assumptions. Every four-position relation on clean positions is exact.</p><p>Fill missing points one at a time, maintaining that assigned points have entry probability at most \\(\\gamma\\) into the current missing set, and that all \\(H\\)-direction relations on assigned points are exact. Lemma 2.1 supplies a missing point \\(x\\) of entry probability at most \\(\\gamma\\); removing it only decreases entry probabilities.</p><p>Fix distinct nonzero \\(a_1,a_2,a_3\\) and interpolation coefficients \\(\\ell_i\\) for evaluation at zero. A direction \\(k\\) predicts\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\ell_iP'(x+a_ik)\n\\]\nwhen its three positions are assigned, and a fixed default otherwise. For independent \\(h,k\\), require six axis points and nine grid points\n\\[\nx+a_ih,\\quad x+a_jk,\\quad x+a_ih+a_jk\n\\]\nto be assigned, and require \\(h,k\\in H\\). Axis failures cost at most \\(6\\gamma\\), grid failures at most \\(9\\gamma\\), and direction failures at most \\(2\\delta\\). On the remaining event, interpolating the grid by rows and columns shows that the predictions agree.</p><p>The prediction distribution therefore has collision probability at least \\(1-15\\gamma-2\\delta\\), which is at most its largest atom. Assign a modal value \\(m_x\\) to \\(x\\). A random prediction equals \\(m_x\\) and uses an old assigned triple except with probability at most \\(18\\gamma+2\\delta\\). At an old assigned center, prediction reproduces its value with an old assigned triple except with probability at most \\(3\\gamma+\\delta\\).</p><p>Consider a relation newly enabled by assigning \\(x\\). A zero direction gives an automatic relation. Otherwise exactly one of its four distinct positions is \\(x\\). A fresh transverse direction gives simultaneous correct interpolation from old triples at all four centers except with probability at most\n\\[\n(18\\gamma+2\\delta)+3(3\\gamma+\\delta)=27\\gamma+5\\delta&lt;1.\n\\]\nChoose such a transverse direction. Its three translated relations lie entirely in the old assigned set and vanish. Interpolating them back proves the new relation for the already fixed \\(m_x\\).</p><p>No union bound over relations or filling steps is used. After all points are assigned, interpolation at three parameters and then any fourth proves degree at most two. Only \\(D\\) was changed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.5", "orig": "Lemma 3.6. Support gap", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Suppose a scalar-invariant set of directions has \\(\\mathcal D\\)-mass at least \\(1-\\rho\\), and \\(F_1:G\\to F\\) has degree at most \\(D_1<q-1\\) on every line in those directions.", "statement": "<p>Suppose a scalar-invariant set of directions has \\(\\mathcal D\\)-mass at least \\(1-\\rho\\), and \\(F_1:G\\to F\\) has degree at most \\(D_1&lt;q-1\\) on every line in those directions. Then either \\(F_1=0\\) identically, or\n\\[\n\\mu(\\operatorname{supp}F_1)\n\\geq1-\\rho-\\frac{D_1}{q-1}-\\lambda _1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>From a support point \\(x\\), sample \\(h\\sim\\mathcal D\\) and independent uniform \\(a\\in F^*\\). In a good direction, the line polynomial is nonzero at zero and has at most \\(D_1\\) roots. Thus \\(x+ah\\) remains in the support with probability at least\n\\[\n1-\\rho-\\frac{D_1}{q-1}.\n\\]\nThe step \\(ah\\) has law \\(\\mathcal D\\). Average over support points and apply Lemma 2.1.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 4.2. Primitive events and a valuation obstruction", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(\\Omega=\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathcal S}\\), where \\(\\mathcal S\\) is the bit space of Proposition 2 with pair bound \\(\\lambda\\leq10^{-6}\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\Omega=\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathcal S}\\), where \\(\\mathcal S\\) is the bit space of Proposition 2 with pair bound \\(\\lambda\\leq10^{-6}\\). Predicates are actual Boolean functions \\(R:\\Omega\\to\\{0,1\\}\\), identified when equal as functions. Write \\(R\\subseteq R'\\) for pointwise inclusion.</p><p>A primitive type is a law \\(P_s\\) on predicates with a calibration \\(p_s\\in[0,1]\\). A specified ordered comparison couples designated primitive marginals and is supported on \\(R\\subseteq R'\\).</p><p>There is a fixed finite family of types and comparisons and a fixed \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\), polynomially constructible from the formula, such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Each predicate has bounded arity; samplers are polynomially enumerable finite rational laws with inverse-polynomial positive seed probabilities.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The constant predicates \\(1,0\\) are types with calibrations \\(1,0\\). Every comparison marginal is a primitive type, and every type has a comparison to \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. At a perfect assignment \\(y^*\\), \\(\\mathbb E_{P_s}R(y^*)=p_s\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. On an unsatisfiable instance there is no Boolean function \\(H\\) of actual predicates satisfying, for every type and comparison,\n\\[\n|\\mathbb E_{P_s}H-p_s|\\leq\\zeta,\n\\qquad\n\\Pr[H(R)&gt;H(R')]\\leq\\zeta.\n\\]\nThese are the calibration and order conditions of original Definition 4.1.</p><p>The types are as follows.</p><p>The basic literal laws are\n\\[\nL_i^a(y)=\\mathbf1_{\\{y_i=a\\}},\n\\qquad\nL_{i,j}^a(y)=\\mathbf1_{\\{y_i+y_j=a\\}},\n\\]\nwith uniform \\(i\\in\\mathcal S\\), uniform \\((i,j)\\in\\mathcal S^2\\), and independent uniform target \\(a\\); both calibrations are \\(1/2\\).</p><p>Use three even-parity expression experiments: the auxiliary four base expressions, four sum expressions from two independent auxiliary tuples, and\n\\[\ny_i,\\ y_j,\\ y_i+y_j\n\\]\nfor independent uniform \\(i,j\\). For experiment length \\(k\\in\\{3,4\\}\\), choose a uniform even target \\(a\\in\\mathbb F_2^k\\), and include its match predicate \\(M\\), complement \\(\\overline M\\), and comparisons\n\\[\nM\\subseteq L^{a_\\ell},\\qquad L^{1-a_\\ell}\\subseteq\\overline M.\n\\]\nTheir calibrations are \\(2^{-(k-1)}\\) and \\(1-2^{-(k-1)}\\).</p><p>For every ordinary test of arity \\(L\\), seed \\(e\\), questions \\(i_1,\\ldots,i_L\\), and acceptance function \\(T_e\\), choose independent uniform \\(a\\in\\mathbb F_2^L\\) and include\n\\[\nR_{e,a}(y)=T_e(a)\\prod_{\\ell=1}^LL_{i_\\ell}^{a_\\ell}(y),\n\\qquad p_s=2^{-L}.\n\\]\nCompare it to every containing literal, and include those literal marginals as types of calibration \\(1/2\\). Their question marginals are at most \\(B\\) times uniform.</p>", "proof": "<p>The displayed predicate laws have the claimed arities and sampling properties by Proposition 2. At a perfect assignment, each parity experiment has an even response tuple, so exactly one of its \\(2^{k-1}\\) targets matches. Every seeded ordinary test passes, so exactly one of \\(2^L\\) targets gives its match-and-pass event. This proves all stated calibrations. Add constant types and comparisons \\((R,1)\\) for every type.</p><p>If a calibrated ordered valuation existed on an unsatisfiable instance, Lemmas 3.1–3.2 would make almost every base question a dictator. Lemma 3.3 would decode one assignment. Choose the fixed \\(\\zeta\\leq10^{-8}\\) small enough that\n\\[\n2^L(1+L+416LB)\\zeta\\leq\\eta/2\n\\]\nfor every ordinary law. There are only finitely many laws. The decoded assignment contradicts Proposition 2.</p><p>For subsequent propositions fix positive rational mixture weights \\(\\omega_s\\), \\(\\sum_s\\omega_s=1\\), and put\n\\[\nP_*=\\sum_s\\omega_sP_s,\\qquad p_*=\\sum_s\\omega_sp_s.\n\\]\nThe constant types ensure \\(0&lt;p_*&lt;1\\). Type and presentation marks may be retained in sampling, but valuations remain functions of actual predicates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Tag–dictator dichotomy; original Section 4.2.** Suppose \\(H\\) satisfies the calibration and order conditions of Proposition 3.", "statement": "<p>**Tag–dictator dichotomy; original Section 4.2.**</p><p>Suppose \\(H\\) satisfies the calibration and order conditions of Proposition 3. For either basic question space, define\n\\[\nB(q,a)=2H(L_q^a)-1,\\qquad\nb(q)=\\frac{B(q,0)+B(q,1)}2.\n\\]\nA question is a tag if its two signs agree, and otherwise is a dictator. Let \\(\\delta\\) be its tag fraction.</p><p>For \\(\\lambda\\leq1/28\\) and \\(\\zeta\\leq1\\),\n\\[\n\\delta\\leq416\\zeta\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n1-\\delta\\leq7\\lambda+208\\zeta.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Calibration gives\n\\[\n|\\mathbb Eb|\\leq2\\zeta,\\qquad \\mathbb Eb^2=\\delta.\n\\]\nIn the four-occurrence experiment, distinct target coordinates are independent. The pair-operator bound gives, for \\(\\ell\\neq r\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb EB_\\ell B_r\n=\\mathbb Eb(q_\\ell)b(q_r)\n\\leq4\\zeta^2+\\lambda\\delta.\n\\]\nThe match comparison gives\n\\[\n\\Pr[B_1=\\cdots=B_4=1]\\geq1/8-5\\zeta.\n\\]\nComplement calibration and the opposite-literal comparisons give the same bound for all signs \\(-1\\), since complementing four targets preserves the even-target law.</p><p>The squared sum of four signs is \\(16\\) on the two constant-sign vectors, \\(4\\) when their product is negative, and zero otherwise. Therefore\n\\[\n\\Pr[B_1B_2B_3B_4=-1]\n\\leq3\\lambda\\delta+40\\zeta+12\\zeta^2\n\\leq3\\lambda\\delta+52\\zeta.\n\\]\nWhen some but not all questions are tags, the product contains a nonempty proper subset of target signs and is unbiased under even targets. The probability that the first question is a tag and the second is not is at least\n\\[\n\\delta(1-\\delta)-\\lambda\\delta.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\delta(1-\\delta)\\leq7\\lambda\\delta+104\\zeta.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\delta\\leq1/2\\) this gives \\(\\delta\\leq416\\zeta\\), and for \\(\\delta&gt;1/2\\) it gives \\(1-\\delta\\leq7\\lambda+208\\zeta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Exclusion of the tag regime; original Section 4.3.** Under Proposition 3, assume \\(\\lambda\\leq10^{-6}\\), \\(\\zeta\\leq10^{-8}\\), and that \\(H\\) satisfies calibration and order.", "statement": "<p>**Exclusion of the tag regime; original Section 4.3.**</p><p>Under Proposition 3, assume \\(\\lambda\\leq10^{-6}\\), \\(\\zeta\\leq10^{-8}\\), and that \\(H\\) satisfies calibration and order. Then the base literal law cannot lie in the second regime of Lemma 3.1. In particular,\n\\[\n\\Pr_{i\\in\\mathcal S}[i\\text{ is not a dictator}]\\leq416\\zeta.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose instead that the base non-tag fraction is at most\n\\[\ne_0=7\\lambda+208\\zeta.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nh_0=e_0+2\\zeta,\\qquad D_0=3e_0+6\\zeta.\n\\]\nExtend the base tag sign to \\(b_i\\in\\{\\pm1\\}\\) arbitrarily. Then \\(|\\mathbb Eb_i|\\leq h_0\\). If \\(r_s=\\Pr[b_i=s]\\), then\n\\[\n|r_s-1/2|\\leq h_0/2,\\qquad r_s^2\\leq1/4+h_0.\n\\]</p><p>In the triple experiment, match calibration and its three comparisons give an all-positive vector with probability at least \\(1/4-4\\zeta\\) under even targets. The complement comparisons give an all-negative vector with the same bound under complemented, odd targets. Outside at most \\(2e_0\\) non-tag base questions, these require \\(b_i=b_j=s\\). The sum target is uniform under either law. Subtracting from \\(r_s^2\\) gives\n\\[\n\\Pr_{i,j,a}[b_i=b_j=s,\\ B((i,j),a)\\neq s]\\leq D_0.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\Pr[b_i=b_j=s,\\ (i,j)\\text{ is not a tag of sign }s]\\leq2D_0.\n\\]\nSince \\(r_+^2+r_-^2\\geq1/2\\), the sum tag fraction is at least \\(1/2-4D_0&gt;416\\zeta\\). Its dichotomy therefore gives sum non-tag fraction at most \\(e_0\\).</p><p>Let \\(v(i,j)\\) extend the sum tag sign symmetrically. Symmetry is permitted because the predicates for \\((i,j)\\) and \\((j,i)\\) are equal. Then\n\\[\n\\Pr[b_i=b_j=s,\\ v(i,j)\\neq s]\\leq2D_0.\n\\]\nFor independent auxiliary four-tuples,\n\\[\n\\Pr\\!\\left[\\prod_{\\ell=1}^4v(i_\\ell,j_\\ell)=-1\\right]\n\\leq E_4:=3\\lambda+52\\zeta+4e_0.\n\\]\nExchangeability permits swapping \\(j_1,j_2\\). Where both products are positive, cancellation yields\n\\[\nv(i_1,j_1)v(i_2,j_2)=v(i_1,j_2)v(i_2,j_1).\n\\]\nThus rectangle failure under auxiliary pair marginals is at most \\(2E_4\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(i,i'\\), apply the pair bound to\n\\[\nf(j)=v(i,j)v(i',j).\n\\]\nReplacing a correlated \\(j\\)-pair by independent uniform draws changes the rectangle-sign expectation by at most \\(\\lambda\\). Replacing the \\(i\\)-pair costs another \\(\\lambda\\). Hence rectangle failure for four independent uniform indices is at most\n\\[\nR_0=2E_4+\\lambda.\n\\]</p><p>Fix a reference pair \\((i_0,j_0)\\) with rectangle failure at most \\(R_0\\), and set\n\\[\nu(i)=v(i,j_0),\\qquad w(j)=v(i_0,j)v(i_0,j_0).\n\\]\nThen \\(\\Pr[v(i,j)\\neq u(i)w(j)]\\leq R_0\\). Symmetry implies\n\\[\n\\Pr[u(i)w(j)\\neq u(j)w(i)]\\leq2R_0.\n\\]\nThe signs \\(a(i)=u(i)w(i)\\) disagree at independent indices with probability at most \\(2R_0\\). Their minority mass is therefore at most \\(2R_0\\). For the majority sign \\(c\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr[v(i,j)\\neq cu(i)u(j)]\\leq3R_0.\n\\]</p><p>Take the base class \\(s=-c\\), whose mass is at least \\(1/4\\). Conditional on two independent draws from that class, \\(u(i)u(j)=1\\) with probability at least \\(1/2\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\Pr[b_i=b_j=s,\\ cu(i)u(j)\\neq s]\\geq1/32.\n\\]\nThe previous bounds upper-bound this probability by\n\\[\n2D_0+3R_0=231\\lambda+6564\\zeta&lt;1/32,\n\\]\na contradiction. The same numerical assumptions ensure the intermediate inequalities \\(h_0&lt;1/2\\) and \\(1/2-4D_0&gt;416\\zeta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Decoding the ordinary tests; original Section 4.4.** Under the assumptions of Lemma 3.2, assign each dictator question its unique bit \\(y_i\\) whose literal has valuation one, and assign arbitrary bits to other …", "statement": "<p>**Decoding the ordinary tests; original Section 4.4.**</p><p>Under the assumptions of Lemma 3.2, assign each dictator question its unique bit \\(y_i\\) whose literal has valuation one, and assign arbitrary bits to other questions. For every ordinary law of arity \\(L\\), the resulting assignment has failure probability at most\n\\[\n2^L(1+L+416LB)\\zeta.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The law queries a non-dictator with probability at most \\(416LB\\zeta\\). Its \\(L\\) containing-literal comparisons fail with total probability at most \\(L\\zeta\\) in the seed-and-target experiment. Since \\(\\zeta&lt;1\\), constant-zero calibration gives \\(H(0)=0\\).</p><p>Outside these bad events, \\(H(R_{e,a})=1\\) implies that all targets match the assigned bits and \\(T_e(a)=1\\). An independent uniform target equals the assigned response vector with probability exactly \\(2^{-L}\\), even with repeated questions. Hence\n\\[\n2^{-L}-\\zeta\n\\leq\\mathbb EH(R_{e,a})\n\\leq2^{-L}\\Pr_e[T_e(y_{i_1},\\ldots,y_{i_L})=1]\n+(416LB+L)\\zeta.\n\\]\nRearrangement proves the bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 5.1. Rare-event criterion", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(\\mathcal X\\) be finite.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal X\\) be finite. Predicates are actual functions \\(\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}\\). Fix a finite type set \\(\\mathcal T\\), laws \\(P_s\\), calibrations \\(p_s\\in[0,1]\\), positive mixture weights \\(\\omega_s\\), and\n\\[\nP_*=\\sum_s\\omega_sP_s,\\qquad\np_*=\\sum_s\\omega_sp_s\\in(0,1).\n\\]\nA designated type \\(\\top\\) is concentrated on \\(1\\) and has \\(p_\\top=1\\).</p><p>Fix a finite list \\(\\mathcal C\\) of comparison couplings \\((R,R')\\), with specified primitive marginals, supported on \\(R\\leq R'\\). Include each comparison \\(P_s\\to1\\).</p><p>For length \\(t\\), a type pattern \\(\\mathbf s=(s_1,\\ldots,s_t)\\) samples independent \\(R_i\\sim P_{s_i}\\), with\n\\[\nr=\\bigwedge_{i=1}^tR_i,\\qquad\np_{\\mathbf s}=\\prod_{i=1}^tp_{s_i}.\n\\]\nA lifted comparison changes one coordinate according to a primitive coupling, sharing all independently sampled fixed-type context predicates.</p><p>Fix \\(b_0,K_0,B_0,\\zeta&gt;0\\). There is \\(t_0\\) such that for every \\(t\\geq t_0\\) there is \\(\\varepsilon_0(t)&gt;0\\) with the following property. Put \\(p=p_*^t\\), and take \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon\\leq\\varepsilon_0(t)\\). If \\(0&lt;v\\leq B_0\\) and a Boolean function \\(W\\) of the actual conjunction satisfies\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr_{P_*^t}[W(r)=1]&amp;\\geq b_0vp,\\\\\n\\Pr_{\\mathbf s}[W(r)=1]&amp;\\leq v(K_0p_{\\mathbf s}+\\varepsilon)\n\\quad\\text{for every pattern},\\\\\n\\Pr[W(r)&gt;W(r')]&amp;\\leq\\varepsilon v\n\\quad\\text{for every lifted comparison},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nthen there is one Boolean valuation \\(H\\) of actual primitive predicates such that\n\\[\n|\\mathbb E_{P_s}H-p_s|&lt;\\zeta,\\qquad\n\\Pr_\\Pi[H(R)&gt;H(R')]&lt;\\zeta\n\\]\nfor every type and comparison.</p><p>All choices are uniform in \\(\\mathcal X\\), primitive support sizes, atom probabilities, and the particular comparison couplings. Types with \\(p_s=0\\) are allowed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Sum every calibration error and comparison error into a nonnegative quantity \\(\\Theta(D)\\). Lemma 4.3 gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_D\\Theta(D)\n\\leq\n\\sum_s\\frac{\\rho_t+R_t}{A\\omega_s}\n+\n\\sum_{\\Pi\\in\\mathcal C}\n\\frac{\\rho_t/\\omega_{s(\\Pi)}+\\rho_t/\\omega_{s'(\\Pi)}+u}{A}.\n\\]\nThe sums have fixed finite length. Since \\(0&lt;p_*&lt;1\\),\n\\[\nt^{-1/2}+tp_*^t\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nChoose \\(t_0\\) so that their contributions are less than \\(\\zeta/2\\), and \\(p_*^{2t}\\leq e^{-1}\\), for every \\(t\\geq t_0\\). Then choose \\(\\varepsilon_0(t)&gt;0\\) to satisfy Lemma 4.1 and make all remaining \\(\\varepsilon/p\\) contributions less than \\(\\zeta/2\\). Shrink it to obtain strict inequality.</p><p>Some positive-\\(Q_j\\) prefix has \\(\\Theta(D)&lt;\\zeta\\). Each constituent error is nonnegative, so \\(H=H_D\\) satisfies every requirement. Its dependence only on actual predicates follows from the definition of \\(U_D\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Entropy normalization; original first part of the proof of Lemma 5.1.** Under Proposition 4, let \\(Y_i\\) be marked primitive samples, let \\( Z=\\Pr_{P_*^t}\\!\\left[W\\!\\left(\\bigwedge_iR(Y_i)\\right)=1\\right], \\) and let …", "statement": "<p>**Entropy normalization; original first part of the proof of Lemma 5.1.**</p><p>Under Proposition 4, let \\(Y_i\\) be marked primitive samples, let\n\\[\nZ=\\Pr_{P_*^t}\\!\\left[W\\!\\left(\\bigwedge_iR(Y_i)\\right)=1\\right],\n\\]\nand let \\(Q\\) be the product law conditioned on this event. Write\n\\[\nI_k=D(Q_k\\Vert P_*^k),\\qquad\nL=\\log(1/p_*),\\quad j=\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor,\\quad m=t-j.\n\\]\nFor \\(t\\geq4\\), if\n\\[\n\\frac{t^2\\varepsilon}{p}\\leq c_0:=\\min\\{b_0/8,1\\},\n\\]\nthere are constants \\(c_v,c_z&gt;0\\) and \\(C_I&lt;\\infty\\), depending only on the fixed numerical data, such that\n\\[\nv\\geq c_v,\\qquad Z\\geq c_zp,\\qquad I_j\\geq jL-C_I.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The conditioned law is exchangeable. The entropy chain rule gives\n\\[\nI_k-I_{k-1}\n=\\mathbb E_{Q_{k-1}}\nD(Q(Y_k\\mid Y_1,\\ldots,Y_{k-1})\\Vert P_*).\n\\]\nThese increments are nondecreasing: extra conditioning increases expected relative entropy by convexity of \\(x\\log x\\), and exchangeability identifies the shorter conditional expression. Therefore\n\\[\nI_j\\leq\\frac jt I_t,\\qquad\nI_{j+1}-I_j\\geq I_j/j.\n\\]\nSince the full conditioned density is \\(1/Z\\),\n\\[\nI_t=\\log(1/Z)\\leq\\log(1/v)+tL-\\log b_0.\n\\]</p><p>Replace the last \\(m\\) predicates successively by \\(1\\). Any change from success to failure violates a lifted comparison. Product-law averaging and a union bound give\n\\[\n\\delta:=\nQ\\!\\left[W\\!\\left(\\bigwedge_{i=1}^jR(Y_i)\\right)=0\\right]\n\\leq\\frac{m\\varepsilon v}{Z}\\leq\\frac{t\\varepsilon}{b_0p}.\n\\]\nAdjoining constant-one predicates does not change the argument of \\(W\\). Averaging its type-pattern upper bound gives\n\\[\nP_*^j\\!\\left[W\\!\\left(\\bigwedge_{i=1}^jR(Y_i)\\right)=1\\right]\n\\leq v(K_0e^{-jL}+\\varepsilon).\n\\]\nCoarsening relative entropy to this event, whose \\(Q_j\\)-probability is \\(1-\\delta\\), gives, with \\(a_v=\\log(1/v)\\),\n\\[\nI_j\\geq\n(1-\\delta)\\bigl(a_v+jL-\\log(K_0+\\varepsilon e^{jL})\\bigr)-\\log2.\n\\]\nThis remains valid if the lower bound on the logarithm is negative.</p><p>The smallness assumption implies\n\\[\n\\delta\\leq1/(8t),\\quad\n\\delta jL\\leq L/16,\\quad\n1-\\delta-j/t\\geq3/8,\\quad\n\\varepsilon e^{jL}\\leq1.\n\\]\nCombining the preceding entropy inequalities,\n\\[\n(1-\\delta-j/t)a_v\n\\leq\\delta jL+(1-\\delta)\\log(K_0+\\varepsilon e^{jL})\n+\\log2-(j/t)\\log b_0.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_0&amp;=L/16+\\log(K_0+1)+\\log2+\\tfrac12|\\log b_0|,\\\\\nA_v&amp;=\\tfrac83R_0,\\qquad c_v=e^{-A_v},\\qquad c_z=b_0c_v.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThen \\(a_v\\leq A_v\\), proving the lower bounds.</p><p>The assumption \\(v\\leq B_0\\) separately gives\n\\[\n(1-\\delta)a_v\\geq-\\max\\{\\log B_0,0\\}.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nI_j\\geq jL-C_I,\n\\quad\nC_I=L/16+\\log(K_0+1)+\\log2+\\max\\{\\log B_0,0\\}.\n\\]\nNo assumption \\(v\\leq1\\) was used. If \\(c_v&gt;B_0\\), the hypotheses are inconsistent.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Common conditional density and order estimates; original middle part of Lemma 5.1.** Use the setting and smallness condition of Lemma 4.1.", "statement": "<p>**Common conditional density and order estimates; original middle part of Lemma 5.1.**</p><p>Use the setting and smallness condition of Lemma 4.1. For a full ordered prefix \\(D=(Y_1,\\ldots,Y_j)\\), put \\(R_D=\\bigwedge_{i=1}^jR(Y_i)\\), and define\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nZ_D&amp;=\\mathbb E_{P_*^m}W\\!\\left(R_D\\wedge\\bigwedge_{i=1}^mR(Y_i)\\right),\\\\\nU_D(R)&amp;=\\mathbb E_{P_*^{m-1}}\nW\\!\\left(R_D\\wedge R\\wedge\\bigwedge_{i=1}^{m-1}R(Y_i)\\right),\\\\\nf_D(R)&amp;=U_D(R)/Z_D\n\\quad\\text{when }Z_D&gt;0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nExpectations over prefixes are under \\(Q_j\\). Then \\(f_D\\) depends only on the actual predicate and satisfies\n\\[\n0\\leq f_D\\leq1/Z_D,\\qquad \\int f_D\\,dP_*=1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_D\\int f_D\\log f_D\\,dP_*\\geq L-C_I/j.\n\\]</p><p>Put\n\\[\n\\pi_s=\\frac{\\omega_sp_s}{p_*},\\quad\nu=\\frac{\\varepsilon}{b_0p},\\quad\nR_t=\\frac{K_0}{2b_0\\sqrt m}+u,\\quad\nA=\\frac1{p_*},\\quad H_{\\max}=\\frac1{\\omega_\\top}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_D|\\omega_s\\mathbb E_{P_s}f_D-\\pi_s|\\leq R_t,\n\\]\n\\[\n0\\leq f_D(1)\\leq H_{\\max},\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_D|f_D(1)-A|\\leq R_t/\\omega_\\top,\n\\]\nand every primitive comparison satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_D\\mathbb E_\\Pi(f_D(R)-f_D(R'))_+\\leq u.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_D\\int(f_D-f_D(1))_+\\,dP_*\\leq u.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The prefix density relative to \\(P_*^j\\) is \\(Z_D/Z\\), so \\(Z_D&gt;0\\) almost surely under \\(Q_j\\). The ratio \\(f_D\\) is the next-coordinate density. The entropy chain rule and Lemma 4.1 give the entropy lower bound.</p><p>For every complete type sequence \\(\\mathbf s\\), the hypotheses give\n\\[\nQ(\\text{types}=\\mathbf s)\n\\leq\\frac{K_0}{b_0}\\prod_{i=1}^t\\pi_{s_i}\n+u\\prod_{i=1}^t\\omega_{s_i}.\n\\]\nFor a fixed type \\(s\\), let \\(F_s\\) be its empirical frequency in the \\(m\\) suffix coordinates. Under \\(\\pi^{\\otimes t}\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E|F_s-\\pi_s|\n\\leq\\sqrt{\\pi_s(1-\\pi_s)/m}\\leq1/(2\\sqrt m).\n\\]\nThe displayed domination and \\(|F_s-\\pi_s|\\leq1\\) give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_Q|F_s-\\pi_s|\\leq R_t.\n\\]\nEven after fixing the complete marked prefix, the suffix remains exchangeable, so\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_Q(F_s\\mid D)=\\omega_s\\mathbb E_{P_s}f_D.\n\\]\nConditional Jensen proves the type estimate. For the constant-one type, this is\n\\[\n\\omega_\\top f_D(1)\\approx A\\omega_\\top,\n\\]\nand its mass is at most one, proving both assertions about \\(f_D(1)\\).</p><p>For a comparison, cancellation of the prefix likelihood gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_D\\mathbb E_\\Pi(f_D(R)-f_D(R'))_+\n&amp;=\\frac1Z\\mathbb E_{D\\sim P_*^j}\n\\mathbf1_{\\{Z_D&gt;0\\}}\n\\mathbb E_\\Pi(U_D(R)-U_D(R'))_+\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\\frac1Z\n\\mathbb E_{D,\\Pi,\\text{suffix}}\n\\mathbf1_{\\{W(R_D\\wedge R\\wedge R_{\\mathrm{suffix}})\n&gt;W(R_D\\wedge R'\\wedge R_{\\mathrm{suffix}})\\}}\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\\varepsilon v/Z\\leq u.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe middle experiment has independent product-mixture context, so it is an average of the lifted-comparison bounds. Averaging comparisons to \\(1\\) gives the last assertion. No density of the comparison coupling relative to a product law is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Clipping and simultaneous thresholding; original final part of Lemma 5.1.** Under Lemma 4.2, define \\( g_D=\\min\\{f_D,f_D(1)\\},\\qquad \\widehat f_D=\\min\\{g_D,A\\},\\qquad H_D(R)=\\mathbf1_{\\{f_D(R)>A/2\\}}. \\) For …", "statement": "<p>**Clipping and simultaneous thresholding; original final part of Lemma 5.1.**</p><p>Under Lemma 4.2, define\n\\[\ng_D=\\min\\{f_D,f_D(1)\\},\\qquad\n\\widehat f_D=\\min\\{g_D,A\\},\\qquad\nH_D(R)=\\mathbf1_{\\{f_D(R)&gt;A/2\\}}.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(t\\),\n\\[\n\\rho_t:=\n\\mathbb E_D\\int|f_D-AH_D|\\,dP_*\n\\leq\nC\\left(t^{-1/2}+tp+(1+t)\\frac{\\varepsilon}{p}\\right),\n\\]\nwhere \\(C\\) depends only on the fixed numerical data.</p><p>Moreover,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_D|\\mathbb E_{P_s}H_D-p_s|\n\\leq\\frac{\\rho_t+R_t}{A\\omega_s},\n\\]\nand, for a comparison with marginals \\(P_s,P_{s'}\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_D\\Pr_\\Pi[H_D(R)&gt;H_D(R')]\n\\leq\n\\frac{\\rho_t/\\omega_s+\\rho_t/\\omega_{s'}+u}{A}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(\\varphi(x)=x\\log x\\), with \\(\\varphi(0)=0\\). The mean losses satisfy\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_D\\int(f_D-g_D)\\,dP_*\\leq u,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_D\\int(g_D-\\widehat f_D)\\,dP_*\n\\leq R_t/\\omega_\\top.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n0\\leq1-\\mathbb E_D\\int\\widehat f_D\\,dP_*\n\\leq u+R_t/\\omega_\\top.\n\\]</p><p>Take \\(\\beta=p^2\\leq e^{-1}\\) and \\(\\mathcal B_\\beta=\\{0&lt;Z_D&lt;\\beta\\}\\). Since \\(f_D\\leq1/Z_D\\) and has mean one,\n\\[\n\\int\\varphi(f_D)_+\\,dP_*\\leq\\log(1/Z_D).\n\\]\nAlso \\(\\varphi(g_D)\\geq-1/e\\). Using the actual prefix density \\(Z_D/Z\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_D\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal B_\\beta}\n\\int(\\varphi(f_D)-\\varphi(g_D))\\,dP_*\n&amp;\\leq\n\\frac{\\beta\\log(1/\\beta)+\\beta/e}{Z}\\\\\n&amp;\\leq T_t:=\\frac p{c_z}(2tL+1/e).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis bounds the signed loss, including possible negative clipped entropy.</p><p>On complementary prefixes \\(f_D\\leq1/\\beta\\), and the upper derivative bound\n\\[\n\\varphi'(x)\\leq M_\\beta:=1+2tL\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n\\varphi(x)-\\varphi(y)\\leq M_\\beta(x-y),\n\\qquad 0\\leq y\\leq x\\leq1/\\beta.\n\\]\nTheir expected loss is at most \\(M_\\beta u\\). In the second clipping, values change only in \\([A,H_{\\max}]\\); with \\(M_H=1+\\log H_{\\max}\\), its entropy loss is at most \\(M_HR_t/\\omega_\\top\\). Therefore\n\\[\n0\\leq\n\\mathbb E_D\\int\\widehat f_D\\log(A/\\widehat f_D)\\,dP_*\n\\leq C_I/j+T_t+M_\\beta u+M_HR_t/\\omega_\\top.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(0\\leq x\\leq A\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\{0,A\\})\\leq2x\\log(A/x).\n\\]\nFor \\(x\\leq A/2\\), use \\(2\\log(A/x)\\geq2\\log2&gt;1\\). For \\(x\\geq A/2\\), put \\(z=x/A\\), use \\(-\\log z\\geq1-z\\), and \\(2z\\geq1\\). Distance to a fixed set is \\(1\\)-Lipschitz. Also, for every \\(x\\geq0\\),\n\\[\n|x-A\\mathbf1_{\\{x&gt;A/2\\}}|=\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\{0,A\\}).\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\rho_t\\leq\n\\frac{2C_I}{j}+2T_t+(1+2M_\\beta)u\n+(1+2M_H)\\frac{R_t}{\\omega_\\top},\n\\]\nwhich has the stated form.</p><p>Positive mixture weights give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_D\\mathbb E_{P_s}|f_D-AH_D|\\leq\\rho_t/\\omega_s.\n\\]\nCombining with Lemma 4.2 proves calibration. Finally,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA\\mathbf1_{\\{H_D(R)&gt;H_D(R')\\}}\n\\leq{}&amp;|AH_D(R)-f_D(R)|\\\\\n&amp;+(f_D(R)-f_D(R'))_+\n+|f_D(R')-AH_D(R')|,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhich proves the order estimate. No \\(p_s\\) is inverted, including when \\(p_s=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Theorem 6.1. Analytic score gap", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the primitive system of Proposition 3.", "statement": "<p>Use the primitive system of Proposition 3. A score is a bounded real function on \\(\\Omega\\), depending on boundedly many proof bits; equal functions are identified. A lookup \\(h\\) is a binary function of actual scores, Borel measurable on each finite table of score values.</p><p>A raw comparison form is a finite positive measure \\(m\\) on score pairs, evaluated by\n\\[\n\\mathcal D(h)=\\int|h(B)-h(B')|\\,dm(B,B').\n\\]\nIts truth cost at \\(x\\in\\Omega\\) is\n\\[\n\\int|B(x)-B'(x)|\\,dm(B,B').\n\\]</p><p>For every fixed \\(T_0\\geq1\\), there are a demand law \\(\\mu\\) and a finite sum \\(\\mathcal D\\) of raw comparison forms, independent of the lookup, such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. All score arities, ranges, numerical dimensions, and total comparison weights are bounded by constants independent of input size.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. At a perfect witness \\(x\\),\n\\[\n\\int|B(x)-B'(x)|\\,dm\\leq4,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mu\\{B:B(x)\\geq1/4\\},\n\\ \\mu\\{B:B(x)\\leq-1/4\\}\\geq10^{-4}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. On an unsatisfiable instance, every measurable binary lookup with\n\\[\n\\nu=\\operatorname{Var}_\\mu(h)&gt;0\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n\\mathcal D(h)&gt;T_0\\nu.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Conditional on discrete sampling data, numerical densities and score entries belong to fixed finite families of rational polynomials on rational boxes, with atoms separated into cases.</p><p>For the construction it suffices to take rational \\(T_0\\). Fix the rational primitive mixture \\(P_*\\), with \\(p_*\\in(0,1)\\). A length-\\(t\\) independent mixture conjunction \\(r\\) has honest probability \\(p=p_*^t\\).</p><p>There are \\(M\\) stages, with even sizes \\(n_j\\), and rational coefficients\n\\[\n\\frac1{\\sqrt{pMn_j}}\\leq s_j\\leq\\frac2{\\sqrt{pMn_j}},\\qquad s_j\\leq1.\n\\]\nEach slot independently samples a conjunction presentation \\(R_i\\), actual predicate \\(r_i\\), and amplitude\n\\[\na_i\\sim\\lambda:=\\tfrac12\\delta_0+\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Unif}[-1,1].\n\\]\nThe demand score is\n\\[\nB^0=\\sum_{j=1}^Ms_j\\sum_{i\\text{ in stage }j}a_ir_i,\n\\qquad \\mu=\\operatorname{Law}(B^0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For rational \\(T_0\\), Lemma 5.2 constructs the demand, comparisons, bounded truth cost, and two honest tails. The tail estimate \\(25/(2\\cdot9216)\\) is greater than \\(10^{-4}\\).</p><p>Suppose a lookup on an unsatisfiable instance had positive variance and \\(\\mathcal D(h)\\leq T_0\\nu\\). Lemma 5.1 supplies the initial amplitude signal; Lemma 5.3 preserves a clipped coarse signal at a good stage. Lemmas 5.4–5.5 convert it to a common actual-predicate response with type and order estimates. Lemma 5.6 supplies one exterior and threshold satisfying Proposition 4. The resulting valuation contradicts Proposition 3. Hence \\(\\mathcal D(h)&gt;T_0\\nu\\).</p><p>All constants are fixed in the order\n\\[\nT_0;\\quad e,B_*,\\gamma,b_0,K_0,B_0;\\quad\nt,\\varepsilon;\\quad\\Delta,M;\\quad\n\\alpha_j,m_j,n_j,s_j,\\xi_j.\n\\]\nNo later stage size enters an earlier path constant. For real \\(T_0\\), use a larger rational value.</p><p>Conditional on finite seed data, all Boolean tables and marks are fixed. Scores are rational polynomial expressions in amplitudes, interpolation times, and noises. Their numerical families and finite enumeration are detailed in Proposition 6.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.2. Amplitude signal", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use the demand construction of Proposition 5.", "statement": "<p>Use the demand construction of Proposition 5. Put\n\\[\n\\rho=\\frac1{2048T_0^2},\\qquad\nk=\\lceil\\rho^{-1}\\rceil,\\qquad c=4^{-(k+2)},\n\\]\nand require \\(n_j\\geq4k\\). Let \\(B^{0,\\rho}\\) independently resample each complete primary slot with probability \\(\\rho\\).</p><p>Suppose\n\\[\n0&lt;\\nu=\\operatorname{Var}(h(B^0)),\\qquad\n\\mathbb E|h(B^0)-h(B^{0,\\rho})|\\leq\\nu/16.\n\\]\nIndependently freeze a uniform half \\(F_j\\) of each stage, and write \\(I_j\\) for its complement. Let \\(\\mathcal Z\\) contain the freeze sets and complete frozen primary variables. Let \\(\\mathcal B\\) additionally contain all unfrozen primary presentations, but no unfrozen amplitudes.</p><p>For \\(i\\in I_j\\), define\n\\[\ns_jb_i^0=\n\\mathbb E[h(B^0)\\mid\\mathcal B,a_i]\n-\\mathbb E[h(B^0)\\mid\\mathcal B],\n\\qquad\ng_i^0=\\mathbb E[b_i^0\\mid\\mathcal Z,R_i,a_i].\n\\]\nFor a family \\(b=(b_i)\\), put\n\\[\n\\|b\\|_\\Sigma^2\n=\\mathbb E\\sum_js_j^2\\sum_{i\\in I_j}|b_i|^2.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\|b^0\\|_\\Sigma^2\\leq\\nu,\\qquad\n\\sum_js_j^2\\sum_{i\\in I_j}\\mathbb E[|b_i^0|^2\\mid\\mathcal B]\\leq1/4,\n\\qquad\n\\|g^0\\|_\\Sigma^2\\geq c\\nu.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For one slot, observe only \\(*\\) when \\(a=0\\), and \\((R,a)\\) otherwise. For a function \\(\\psi\\) of this observation, write \\(c_*=\\psi(*)\\), \\(\\psi_1(R,u)\\) for nonerased values, and \\(m=\\mathbb E\\psi_1\\). Then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(\\mathbb E[\\psi\\mid R])\n=\\tfrac14\\operatorname{Var}(\\mathbb E_u\\psi_1)\n\\leq\\tfrac14\\operatorname{Var}(\\psi_1),\n\\]\nwhereas\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(\\psi)\n=\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Var}(\\psi_1)+\\tfrac14(c_*-m)^2.\n\\]\nThus the centered conditional expectation operator to \\(R\\) has norm at most \\(2^{-1/2}\\).</p><p>The centered function \\(f=h(B^0)-\\mathbb Eh(B^0)\\) depends on independent erasure observations. Its orthogonal product-space decomposition by active sets \\(S\\neq\\varnothing\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbb E[f\\mid(R_i)_i]\\|_2^2\n\\leq\\sum_{S\\neq\\varnothing}2^{-|S|}\\|f_S\\|_2^2\n\\leq\\nu/2.\n\\]</p><p>On complete slots let \\(P_i\\) average the slot and \\(\\mathsf R_i\\) average only its amplitude. The commuting orthogonal projections\n\\[\nC_i=P_i,\\qquad D_i=\\mathsf R_i-P_i,\\qquad A_i=I-\\mathsf R_i\n\\]\ndecompose \\(f\\) as \\(f_\\eta\\), where each coordinate uses \\(C_i,D_i\\), or \\(A_i\\). Write\n\\[\nS(\\eta)=\\{i:\\eta_i\\neq C_i\\},\n\\qquad A(\\eta)=\\{i:\\eta_i=A_i\\}.\n\\]\nThe preceding bound implies squared mass at least \\(\\nu/2\\) on \\(A(\\eta)\\neq\\varnothing\\). Slot resampling gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(h(B^0)-h(B^{0,\\rho}))^2\n=2\\sum_\\eta[1-(1-\\rho)^{|S(\\eta)|}]\\|f_\\eta\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nFor \\(|S(\\eta)|&gt;k\\), the coefficient is at least one. Binary absolute and squared differences coincide, so\n\\[\n\\sum_{A(\\eta)\\neq\\varnothing,\\ |S(\\eta)|\\leq k}\\|f_\\eta\\|_2^2\n\\geq3\\nu/8.\n\\]</p><p>Given \\(\\mathcal B\\), the unfrozen amplitudes are independent. The \\(s_jb_i^0\\) are separate centered amplitude projections. Bessel's inequality bounds their summed conditional squared norms by\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(h(B^0)\\mid\\mathcal B)\\leq1/4.\n\\]\nAveraging and total variance prove the first two assertions.</p><p>The tower property gives\n\\[\ns_jg_i^0=\n\\mathbb E[h(B^0)\\mid\\mathcal Z,R_i,a_i]\n-\\mathbb E[h(B^0)\\mid\\mathcal Z,R_i].\n\\]\nFor a fixed freeze set, this is the projection\n\\[\nA_i\\prod_{\\ell\\notin F\\cup\\{i\\}}P_\\ell f.\n\\]\nOrthogonality therefore yields\n\\[\n\\|g^0\\|_\\Sigma^2\n=\\sum_\\eta\\|f_\\eta\\|_2^2\n\\sum_{i\\in A(\\eta)}\n\\Pr_F[i\\notin F,\\ S(\\eta)\\setminus\\{i\\}\\subseteq F].\n\\]</p><p>For a component of degree \\(d\\leq k\\), distinguish \\(i\\in A(\\eta)\\) in stage \\(j\\), and let \\(q_\\ell\\) count active slots in stage \\(\\ell\\). Its capture probability is\n\\[\n\\frac12\n\\frac{(n_j/2)_{q_j-1}}{(n_j-1)_{q_j-1}}\n\\prod_{\\ell\\neq j}\\frac{(n_\\ell/2)_{q_\\ell}}{(n_\\ell)_{q_\\ell}}.\n\\]\nEvery ratio factor is at least \\(1/4\\), because \\(n_\\ell\\geq4k\\). Thus it is at least \\(4^{-k}\\). Keeping one distinguished contribution proves \\(\\|g^0\\|_\\Sigma^2\\geq c\\nu\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Comparison measures and sequential funding; original Sections 6.2–6.5.** Use Proposition 5 and the constants \\(k,c\\) of Lemma 5.1.", "statement": "<p>**Comparison measures and sequential funding; original Sections 6.2–6.5.**</p><p>Use Proposition 5 and the constants \\(k,c\\) of Lemma 5.1. There are choices of all remaining constants, replacement experiments, smoothing laws, and positive raw comparison forms with total honest truth cost at most four, such that any lookup satisfying\n\\[\n0&lt;\\nu=\\operatorname{Var}(h(B^0)),\\qquad \\mathcal D(h)\\leq T_0\\nu\n\\]\nobeys the estimates below.</p><p>Choose rational constants\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nc_1=c/4,\\quad 0&lt;e\\leq c/160000,\\quad\nB_*\\geq\\max\\{1,3200(T_0+1)/c\\},\\\\\nK_*=32(T_0+2),\\quad\n0&lt;\\gamma\\leq c_1/(16K_*),\\\\\nb_*=\\frac{c_1}{128B_*^2},\\quad\nb_0=\\frac{b_*}{8(T_0+1)},\\quad K_0=16/\\gamma,\\quad B_0=B_*.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nApply Proposition 4 with these constants and Proposition 3's tolerance, choosing \\(t\\) and rational \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\). Put \\(p=p_*^t\\). Let \\(V\\) count all fixed type-pattern laws and the iid conjunction law, and \\(Q\\) count all lifted ordered couplings. Set\n\\[\nL=V+2Q+2,\\qquad A_t=16V+80Q,\n\\]\nand choose rational \\(\\Delta&gt;0\\) and integer \\(M\\) so that\n\\[\n\\Delta\\leq b_*p\\gamma/160,\\qquad\n\\left(\\frac{16p}{\\gamma\\varepsilon}+1\\right)\n\\frac{A_t\\Delta}{\\gamma}\\leq b_*p/4,\n\\]\n\\[\nM\\geq\\frac{32B_*K_*(T_0+3)}{c_1\\Delta}.\n\\]</p><p>Starting with the one-cell partition \\(\\mathcal C_0\\), stage \\(j\\) replaces each unfrozen slot independently with probability \\(\\alpha_j\\). A replacement chooses a uniform one of these \\(L\\) paths:\n\\[\n-\\tau r\\to+\\tau r,\n\\]\nfor each vertical law;\n\\[\n+\\tau r\\to+\\tau r',\\qquad-\\tau r'\\to-\\tau r,\n\\]\nfor each ordered coupling; and\n\\[\n-\\tau r\\to ar,\\qquad ar\\to+\\tau r\n\\]\nfor iid \\(r\\) and fresh \\(a\\sim\\lambda\\).\nHere \\(\\tau\\) is a uniform sign descriptor on the formal cells of \\(\\mathcal C_{j-1}\\). With uniform \\(u\\in[0,1]\\) and fair \\(\\chi\\in\\{\\pm1\\}\\), the replacing summand is\n\\[\ns_j\\chi[(1-u)D^-+uD^+].\n\\]\nA retained trial is called shared. Let \\(B^j\\) be the score after replacements through stage \\(j\\).</p><p>Refine \\(\\mathcal C_{j-1}\\) by current unfrozen primary question lists and fresh path question lists. Use fixed orders, zero padding, and retain inconsistent and empty formal cells, so \\(J_j=|\\mathcal C_j|\\) is deterministic. Do not use numerical amplitudes, times, or signs to refine.</p><p>Let \\(S_j\\) be the first \\(m_j\\) indices in \\(I_j\\). Delete their current summands to obtain \\(B_\\circ^j\\); delete only slot \\(i\\) to obtain \\(B_{\\neg i}^j\\).</p><p>For rational \\(\\xi&gt;0\\), use\n\\[\nf_\\xi(a)=\\frac{15}{16\\xi}(1-a^2/\\xi^2)^2\\mathbf1_{[-\\xi,\\xi]}(a).\n\\]\nIndependent cell noises define \\(\\Theta_{\\leq j}\\), and\n\\[\nh_j(B)=\\mathbb E_\\theta h(B+\\Theta_{\\leq j}),\\qquad\nK_{j,z}(B)=\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial a}h_j(B+a\\mathbf1_z)\\right|_{a=0}.\n\\]\nWrite \\(K_j=K_j(B^j)\\), and let \\(\\pi\\) sum child coordinates on each parent.</p><p>Set\n\\[\n\\sigma^j=\\operatorname{sign}K_{j-1}(B_\\circ^j),\\qquad\n\\beta_j=\\frac{\\alpha_j}{2L2^{J_{j-1}}},\\qquad\na_j=\\min\\{p,\\Delta\\beta_j\\}.\n\\]\nThe descriptor \\(\\sigma^j\\) is used only in analysis.</p><p>The resulting estimates are\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\mathbb E|K_M|_1\\leq T_0\\nu,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{z\\text{ marked}}|K_{j,z}|\\leq a_j\\nu,\\\\\n\\mathbb E|\\pi K_j-K_{j-1}|_1\\leq\\nu/M,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E|\\pi K_j-K_{j-1}(B_\\circ^j)|_1\\leq\\nu/M,\\\\\n\\mathbb E|h(B^0)-h_{j-1}(B^j)|^2\\leq e\\nu/M.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nFor a uniform slot forced shared,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E|h_j(B^j)-h_{j-1}(B^j)|\n\\leq ep s_j^2\\nu.\n\\]\nFor each mode, each endpoint \\(B=B_{\\neg i}^j+s_jD^\\pm\\), and a uniform forced slot in \\(S_j\\) with independent uniform descriptor and \\(\\chi=1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E|h_j(B)-h_{j-1}(B)|\n\\leq s_j\\Delta2^{-J_{j-1}}\\nu.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At truth, demand summands are independent, symmetric, centered, bounded by one, and have variance \\(s_j^2p/6\\). Thus\n\\[\n1/6\\leq\\operatorname{Var}(B^0(x))\\leq2/3,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb EB^0(x)^4\n\\leq\\sum_{j,i}\\mathbb E(s_ja_ir_i(x))^2\n+3\\operatorname{Var}(B^0(x))^2&lt;4.\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\Pr[|B^0(x)|\\geq1/4]\n\\geq\\frac{(\\mathbb EB^0(x)^2-1/16)^2}{\\mathbb EB^0(x)^4}\n\\geq25/9216.\n\\]\nSymmetry splits this equally between the tails.</p><p>Include the resampling comparison with weight \\(16T_0\\). Its truth cost is at most\n\\[\n16T_0\\sqrt{2\\rho\\operatorname{Var}(B^0(x))}\n\\leq1/\\sqrt6&lt;1.\n\\]\nLow comparison cost implies the hypothesis of Lemma 5.1.</p><p>Conditional on frozen variables and previous trials, stage summand changes are independent and centered, with second moments at most \\(4s_j^2\\alpha_j\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\mathbb E|B^j(x)-B^{j-1}(x)|\n\\leq\\delta_j:=4\\sqrt{\\alpha_j/(pM)}.\n\\]</p><p>The noise density is even, continuously differentiable after extension by zero, and decreasing on its positive half. Direct integration gives\n\\[\n\\int|f_\\xi'|=\\frac{15}{8\\xi},\n\\qquad\n\\int_0^\\xi2b[-f_\\xi'(b)]\\,db=1.\n\\]\nTranslation is continuously differentiable in \\(L^1\\), so the smoothed lookup has all stated cell derivatives even for merely measurable \\(h\\). Empty-cell derivatives vanish.</p><p>A consistent fine cell is marked if:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Some mode-descriptor match fraction in \\(S_j\\), with \\(\\chi=1\\), exceeds \\(2\\beta_j\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Some vertical mode-descriptor hit-match fraction exceeds \\(2\\beta_jp_{\\mathrm{law}}\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The shared primary-hit fraction in \\(I_j\\) exceeds \\(2p\\).</p><p>Every descriptor is tested. Thus empirical bounds hold on unmarked cells for the eventual analytic descriptor \\(\\sigma^j\\), without independence of gradients and counts. If \\(p_{\\min}\\) is the minimum positive vertical honest probability, Chebyshev and a union bound give at a fixed witness\n\\[\n\\Pr[x\\text{ lies in a marked cell}]\n\\leq\\frac{2L2^{J_{j-1}}}{m_j\\beta_jp_{\\min}}\n+\\frac2{n_jp}.\n\\]\nZero-mean counts are zero almost surely.</p><p>For a set \\(A\\) of level-\\(j\\) cells, include the variation form\n\\[\nN_j(B;A)=\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{z\\in A}\\int_0^{\\xi_j}\n[-f_{\\xi_j}'(b)]\n|h(B+\\Theta_{\\leq j}^{z,+b})\n-h(B+\\Theta_{\\leq j}^{z,-b})|\\,db.\n\\]\nPairing positive and negative noise values shows\n\\[\n|K_{j,z}(B)|\n\\leq\n\\int_0^{\\xi_j}[-f_{\\xi_j}'(b)]\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{other}}|h(B+\\Theta^{z,+b})-h(B+\\Theta^{z,-b})|\\,db.\n\\]\nIts truth cost is at most the probability that the witness cell is in \\(A\\). Include \\(N_M(B^M;\\mathcal C_M)\\) with weight one and each marked-cell form with weight \\(T_0/a_j\\).</p><p>For \\(A=B^{j-1}\\) and \\(A=B_\\circ^j\\), include with weight \\(T_0M\\)\n\\[\nP_j(A)=\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{z\\in\\mathcal C_{j-1}}\n\\int_{-\\xi_{j-1}}^{\\xi_{j-1}}\n|f_{\\xi_{j-1}}'(b)|\n|h(B^j+\\Theta_{&lt;j}^{z,b}+\\Theta_j)\n-h(A+\\Theta_{&lt;j}^{z,b})|\\,db.\n\\]\nDifferentiating the preceding noise coordinate shifts all its children equally, so\n\\[\n\\mathbb E|\\pi K_j-K_{j-1}(A)|_1\\leq P_j(A).\n\\]\nWith\n\\[\n\\Lambda_{j-1}=\\frac{15J_{j-1}}{8\\xi_{j-1}},\n\\]\nthe truth costs are at most\n\\[\n\\Lambda_{j-1}(\\delta_j+\\xi_j),\\qquad\n\\Lambda_{j-1}(m_js_j+\\xi_j).\n\\]</p><p>Also include:\n\\[\nB^0\\ \\text{versus}\\ B^j+\\Theta_{&lt;j}\n\\quad\\text{with weight }T_0M/e;\n\\]\nthe uniform forced-shared fine/coarse comparison with weight\n\\[\nT_0/(ep s_j^2);\n\\]\nand, for each mode and each endpoint, the uniform-descriptor forced endpoint fine/coarse comparison with weight\n\\[\nT_0/(s_j\\Delta2^{-J_{j-1}}).\n\\]\nRebuild the fine partition using the forced fresh path data and retain the original primary question list. Their unweighted truth costs are respectively at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{k\\leq j}\\delta_k+\\sum_{\\ell&lt;j}\\xi_\\ell,\\qquad \\xi_j,\\qquad \\xi_j.\n\\]</p><p>Choose parameters sequentially. Put\n\\[\n\\eta_{\\mathrm{fund}}=\\frac1{100M(L+1)},\n\\qquad\nd_{\\mathrm{cap}}=\\frac{\\eta_{\\mathrm{fund}}e}{4T_0M^2},\n\\]\nand choose rational \\(0&lt;\\xi_0\\leq d_{\\mathrm{cap}}\\). At stage \\(j\\), \\(J=J_{j-1}\\) and \\(\\Lambda=\\Lambda_{j-1}\\) are already fixed.</p><p>First choose rational\n\\[\n0&lt;\\alpha_j\\leq\\min\\{1/2,e\\}\n\\]\nso that\n\\[\n\\delta_j\\leq\n\\min\\{d_{\\mathrm{cap}},\\eta_{\\mathrm{fund}}/(2T_0M\\Lambda)\\}.\n\\]\nThen choose\n\\[\nm_j\\geq\n\\frac{2T_0(2L2^J)}\n{\\eta_{\\mathrm{fund}}a_j\\beta_jp_{\\min}},\n\\]\nand even\n\\[\nn_j\\geq\\max\\left\\{\n2m_j,4k,\\frac4{pM},\n\\frac{4T_0}{\\eta_{\\mathrm{fund}}a_jp},\n\\frac{16T_0^2M\\Lambda^2m_j^2}{\\eta_{\\mathrm{fund}}^2p}\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nChoose rational \\(s_j\\) in the required interval, and finally\n\\[\n0&lt;\\xi_j\\leq\n\\min\\left\\{\nd_{\\mathrm{cap}},\n\\frac{\\eta_{\\mathrm{fund}}}{2T_0M\\Lambda},\n\\frac{\\eta_{\\mathrm{fund}}ep s_j^2}{T_0},\n\\frac{\\eta_{\\mathrm{fund}}s_j\\Delta2^{-J}}{T_0}\n\\right\\}.\n\\]</p><p>The marked form costs at most \\(\\eta_{\\mathrm{fund}}\\). Each projected-gradient and forced form costs at most \\(\\eta_{\\mathrm{fund}}\\); for deletion, the lower bound on \\(n_j\\) gives\n\\[\nm_js_j\\leq\\eta_{\\mathrm{fund}}/(2T_0M\\Lambda).\n\\]\nEach cumulative comparison costs at most \\(\\eta_{\\mathrm{fund}}/2\\). There are \\(5+2L\\) tunable forms per stage, so their total cost is less than one. Resampling and final variation add less than two, giving in particular total cost at most four.</p><p>Dividing \\(\\mathcal D(h)\\leq T_0\\nu\\) by the respective weights proves the stated estimates. For the squared estimate, average the raw comparison and use \\(|u-v|^2\\leq|u-v|\\) on \\([0,1]\\). Every parameter is chosen using only earlier parameters, and every measure uses uniform descriptors rather than analytic reference signs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Orientation and clipping; original Section 6.6.** Fix the experiments and measures of Lemma 5.2, and suppose \\( 0<\\nu=\\operatorname{Var}(h(B^0)),\\qquad \\mathcal D(h)\\leq T_0\\nu. \\) Let \\(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal B\\) be as …", "statement": "<p>**Orientation and clipping; original Section 6.6.**</p><p>Fix the experiments and measures of Lemma 5.2, and suppose\n\\[\n0&lt;\\nu=\\operatorname{Var}(h(B^0)),\\qquad \\mathcal D(h)\\leq T_0\\nu.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal B\\) be as in Lemma 5.1. Define\n\\[\nU=\\max_{0\\leq j\\leq M}\\mathbb E[|K_j|_1\\mid\\mathcal B],\n\\qquad v=\\mathbb E[U\\mid\\mathcal Z].\n\\]\nFor \\(z\\in\\mathcal C_j\\), put\n\\[\nd_{j,z}=|K_{j,z}|-\\sigma^j_{\\operatorname{par}(z)}K_{j,z}\\geq0.\n\\]\nCall stage \\(j\\) good if \\(\\mathbb E\\sum_zd_{j,z}\\leq\\Delta\\nu\\).</p><p>For \\(i\\in I_j\\), let \\(b_i\\) be the centered amplitude component, divided by \\(s_j\\), of \\(h_{j-1}(B^j)\\) when \\(i\\) is forced shared, and put\n\\[\ng_i=\\mathbb E[b_i\\mid\\mathcal Z,R_i,a_i].\n\\]\nThen some good stage satisfies\n\\[\n\\Pr_{\\mathcal Z,\\ i\\text{ uniform in }I_j,\\ R_i,a_i}\n[|g_i|\\geq\\gamma,\\ v\\leq B_*]\\geq b_*p\\nu.\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\n\\mathbb Ev\\leq(T_0+1)\\nu.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The pointwise inequality\n\\[\n|K_{j-1}|_1\\leq|K_j|_1+|\\pi K_j-K_{j-1}|_1\n\\]\nand Lemma 5.2 imply\n\\[\n\\mathbb EU=\\mathbb Ev\\leq(T_0+1)\\nu.\n\\]\nFor vectors \\(a,b\\) and \\(\\sigma=\\operatorname{sign}b\\),\n\\[\n|a|_1-\\sigma\\cdot a\\leq2|a-b|_1.\n\\]\nUsing \\(a=\\pi K_j\\), \\(b=K_{j-1}(B_\\circ^j)\\), and the across-stage estimate,\n\\[\n\\sum_zd_{j,z}\n\\leq|K_j|_1-|K_{j-1}|_1\n+|\\pi K_j-K_{j-1}|_1\n+2|\\pi K_j-K_{j-1}(B_\\circ^j)|_1.\n\\]\nSumming telescopes and gives\n\\[\n\\sum_j\\mathbb E\\sum_zd_{j,z}\\leq(T_0+3)\\nu.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(b_i^+\\) be the analogous amplitude component of the fine smoothing \\(h_j(B^j)\\). On replacement, score and partition ignore the primary amplitude \\(a_i\\); hence the ordinary coarse amplitude projection is \\((1-\\alpha_j)s_jb_i\\). Conditional Bessel inequalities applied to the approximation to \\(h(B^0)\\) yield\n\\[\n\\|((1-\\alpha_j)b_i-b_i^0)_i\\|_\\Sigma^2\\leq e\\nu.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\alpha_j\\leq e\\leq1/2\\),\n\\[\n\\|b-b^0\\|_\\Sigma\\leq4\\sqrt{e\\nu}.\n\\]\nProjection contraction and the forced-shared fine/coarse estimate, using\n\\[\n|I_j|s_j^2\\leq2/(pM),\n\\]\ngive\n\\[\n\\|b^+-b\\|_\\Sigma\\leq\\sqrt{2e\\nu}.\n\\]</p><p>Fine differentiation is legitimate because \\(r_i\\) is constant on \\(\\mathcal C_j\\). For every amplitude \\(a\\),\n\\[\n|b_i^+(a)|\n\\leq\n\\frac4{1-\\alpha_j}\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\n\\mathbf1_{\\{i\\text{ shared}\\}}\n\\sum_z|K_{j,z}|r_i(z)\\,\\middle|\\,\\mathcal B\n\\right]\n\\leq8U.\n\\]\nIndeed, bound a centered amplitude value by the integral of absolute derivatives on \\([-1,1]\\); Lebesgue measure is bounded by \\(4\\lambda\\), giving the factor four.</p><p>Averaging over \\(I_j\\), the shared-hit fraction is at most \\(2p\\) on unmarked cells and at most one on marked cells. Therefore\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\operatorname{avg}_{i\\in I_j}|b_i^+|\n\\leq16p(T_0+1)\\nu+8p\\nu\\leq K_*p\\nu.\n\\]</p><p>Let\n\\[\nA_{\\mathrm{clip}}=\\{U\\leq B_*,\\ v\\leq B_*\\},\n\\qquad\n\\widetilde b_i=b_i^+\\mathbf1_{A_{\\mathrm{clip}}},\n\\qquad\n\\widetilde g_i=\\mathbb E[\\widetilde b_i\\mid\\mathcal Z,R_i,a_i].\n\\]\nThis event is \\(\\mathcal B\\)-measurable. The conditional cap from Lemma 5.1 and Markov give\n\\[\n\\|b^0\\mathbf1_{A_{\\mathrm{clip}}^c}\\|_\\Sigma^2\n\\leq\\Pr(A_{\\mathrm{clip}}^c)\n\\leq2(T_0+1)\\nu/B_*.\n\\]\nProjection contraction and the preceding norm comparisons yield\n\\[\n\\|\\widetilde g-g^0\\|_\\Sigma\n\\leq\\left(6\\sqrt e+\\sqrt{2(T_0+1)/B_*}\\right)\\sqrt\\nu,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|\\widetilde g-g\\|_\\Sigma\n\\leq\\left(10\\sqrt e+\\sqrt{2(T_0+1)/B_*}\\right)\\sqrt\\nu.\n\\]\nThese brackets are at most \\(\\sqrt c/25\\) and \\(\\sqrt c/20\\).</p><p>For every family \\(q_i\\), the coefficient bounds give\n\\[\n\\frac p2\\|q\\|_\\Sigma^2\n\\leq\n\\operatorname{avg}_j\\mathbb E\\operatorname{avg}_{i\\in I_j}|q_i|^2\n\\leq2p\\|q\\|_\\Sigma^2.\n\\]\nUsing Lemma 5.1,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{avg}_j\\mathbb E\\operatorname{avg}_{i\\in I_j}\n|\\widetilde g_i|^2\\geq c_1p\\nu,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{avg}_j\\mathbb E\\operatorname{avg}_{i\\in I_j}\n|\\widetilde g_i-g_i|^2\\leq c_1p\\nu/10.\n\\]\nFurthermore \\(|\\widetilde g_i|\\leq8B_*\\), its stagewise averaged first moment is at most \\(K_*p\\nu\\), and it vanishes when \\(v&gt;B_*\\).</p><p>There are at most \\((T_0+3)/\\Delta\\) bad stages. Each contributes at most \\(8B_*K_*p\\nu\\) to the unaveraged second moment. The choice of \\(M\\) bounds their average by \\(c_1p\\nu/4\\). On \\(|g_i|&lt;\\gamma\\),\n\\[\n|\\widetilde g_i|^2\n\\leq2\\gamma|\\widetilde g_i|+|\\widetilde g_i-g_i|^2,\n\\]\nwhose average is at most \\((1/8+1/10)c_1p\\nu\\). At least \\(c_1p\\nu/2\\) remains on good stages with \\(|g_i|\\geq\\gamma\\) and \\(v\\leq B_*\\). Since \\(|\\widetilde g_i|^2\\leq64B_*^2\\), some good stage has the required probability.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Conditional kernels and forced-state identities; original Sections 7.1–7.2.** Fix the good stage of Lemma 5.3, abbreviating \\( s=s_j,\\quad\\alpha=\\alpha_j,\\quad\\beta=\\beta_j,\\quad I=I_j,\\quad S=S_j,\\quad J=J_{j-1}. \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Conditional kernels and forced-state identities; original Sections 7.1–7.2.**</p><p>Fix the good stage of Lemma 5.3, abbreviating\n\\[\ns=s_j,\\quad\\alpha=\\alpha_j,\\quad\\beta=\\beta_j,\\quad\nI=I_j,\\quad S=S_j,\\quad J=J_{j-1}.\n\\]\nFor each exterior \\(\\mathcal Z=z\\), there is a common deleted-slot kernel \\(\\kappa_z(dB,d\\mathcal C)\\), independent of a fresh current primary pair, and a common extended kernel \\(\\kappa_z^S(dB,d\\mathcal C,d\\sigma)\\) for slots in \\(S\\), whose \\((B,\\mathcal C)\\)-marginal is \\(\\kappa_z\\).</p><p>Writing \\(\\varphi_{\\mathcal C}\\) for smoothing through the preceding partitions, define\n\\[\ns\\,g_z(r,a)=\n\\int\\left[\n\\varphi_{\\mathcal C}(B+sar)\n-\\int\\varphi_{\\mathcal C}(B+sa'r)\\,d\\lambda(a')\n\\right]d\\kappa_z(B,\\mathcal C),\n\\]\n\\[\nF_z(r)=\n\\int\\frac{\n\\varphi_{\\mathcal C}(B+s\\sigma r)\n-\\varphi_{\\mathcal C}(B-s\\sigma r)}s\n\\,d\\kappa_z^S(B,\\mathcal C,\\sigma).\n\\]\nThen \\(g_z(r_i,a_i)\\) is a version of every \\(g_i\\), and both functions depend only on the actual predicate \\(r\\). In particular \\(g_z(0,a)=0\\).</p><p>For any mode, forcing a uniform slot in \\(S\\) to that mode, descriptor \\(\\sigma\\), and \\(\\chi=1\\), while rebuilding its fine partition, gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{avg}_{i\\in S}\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{forced}}\\sum_zd_z\n\\leq4\\Delta\\nu.\n\\]\nFor a vertical law,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{avg}_{i\\in S}\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{forced}}\n\\left[\\sum_z|K_{j,z}|r_i^{\\mathrm{path}}(z)\\,\\middle|\\,\\mathcal Z\\right]\n\\leq\n2p_{\\mathrm{law}}\\mathbb E[|K_j|_1\\mid\\mathcal Z]\n+\\beta^{-1}\n\\mathbb E\\left[\\sum_{z\\text{ marked}}|K_{j,z}|\n\\,\\middle|\\,\\mathcal Z\\right].\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Given \\(\\mathcal Z=z\\), let \\(Y_i=(R_i,a_i)\\) for \\(i\\in I\\), with common law \\(P_Y\\). Let \\(X\\) contain earlier unfrozen primary samples and actual trials, all future primary samples, and frozen contributions. It determines the preceding partition sequence \\(\\mathcal C^-\\) and outside score \\(B_{\\mathrm{out}}\\). It contains no analytic reference signs. Conditional on \\(z\\), it is independent of current unfrozen primary samples.</p><p>For preceding partitions \\(\\mathcal C\\), the current trial kernel is\n\\[\nq_{\\mathcal C}\n=(1-\\alpha)\\delta_{\\mathrm{shared}}\n+\\frac\\alpha L\\sum_{m=1}^LP_m(dE)\\,\n\\operatorname{Unif}(\\{\\pm1\\}^J)(d\\tau)\\,\n\\operatorname{Unif}[0,1](du)\\,\n\\operatorname{Unif}\\{\\pm1\\}(d\\chi).\n\\]\nThe exact conditional factorization is\n\\[\nP_z(dX,dY,dT)\n=q_z(dX)\\prod_{i\\in I}\n[P_Y(dY_i)q_{\\mathcal C^-(X)}(dT_i)].\n\\]\nOnly conditional-on-\\(X\\) independence is used.</p><p>If \\(V_{\\mathcal C}(Y_i,T_i)\\) is the slot summand including \\(s\\), then\n\\[\nB_{\\neg i}^j=B_{\\mathrm{out}}+\n\\sum_{k\\in I\\setminus\\{i\\}}V_{\\mathcal C^-}(Y_k,T_k),\n\\]\n\\[\nB_\\circ^j=B_{\\mathrm{out}}+\n\\sum_{k\\in I\\setminus S}V_{\\mathcal C^-}(Y_k,T_k).\n\\]\nThe pair \\((B_{\\neg i}^j,\\mathcal C^-)\\) uses none of \\(Y_i\\), and its conditional law is common to all \\(i\\in I\\) by permutation invariance. This is \\(\\kappa_z\\). The current fine partition is absent.</p><p>Forcing \\(i\\) shared preserves the background law. The tower property and amplitude independence identify its centered amplitude projection with the displayed formula for \\(g_z\\). The formula depends on \\(R_i\\) only through \\(r_i\\), even if a presentation includes unused bit names.</p><p>For \\(i\\in S\\), append\n\\[\n\\sigma=\\operatorname{sign}K_{j-1}(B_\\circ^j).\n\\]\nThis still uses none of \\(Y_i\\), is unchanged when \\(T_i\\) is redrawn, and is common to all \\(i\\in S\\). This gives \\(\\kappa_z^S\\) and the common background for \\(ar,-\\sigma r,+\\sigma r\\).</p><p>For a mode \\(m\\) and slot \\(i\\in S\\), let \\(\\mathcal H_i\\) retain \\(z,X\\), all current primary pairs, and all current trials except \\(T_i\\). It fixes \\(\\sigma\\). The event\n\\[\nE_i=\\{T_i\\text{ is replacement in mode }m,\\ \\tau_i=\\sigma,\\ \\chi_i=1\\}\n\\]\nhas exact conditional probability\n\\[\n\\Pr(E_i\\mid\\mathcal H_i)=\\alpha/(2L2^J)=\\beta.\n\\]\nConditional on this event, path data retain the original mode law and \\(u\\) is independent uniform.</p><p>Let \\(\\Gamma(\\mathcal H_i,T_i)\\) rebuild the entire fine state using the new path questions and unchanged primary lists. For any nonnegative full-state function \\(Q_0\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{forced}}[Q_0(\\Gamma)\\mid\\mathcal H_i]\n=\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{actual}}[Q_0(\\Gamma)\\mathbf1_{E_i}/\\beta\n\\mid\\mathcal H_i].\n\\]\nNo conditioning on the output fine partition is imposed.</p><p>Averaging over \\(i\\in S\\) produces one actual state with common fine gradient, marks, and descriptor. Let\n\\[\nf_m=\\operatorname{avg}_{i\\in S}\\mathbf1_{E_i},\n\\qquad d_z=|K_{j,z}|-\\sigma_{\\operatorname{par}(z)}K_{j,z}.\n\\]\nOn unmarked cells \\(f_m\\leq2\\beta\\); everywhere \\(f_m\\leq1\\) and \\(d_z\\leq2|K_{j,z}|\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{avg}_{i\\in S}\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{forced}}\\sum_zd_z\n&amp;=\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{actual}}\\sum_zd_z\\,f_m/\\beta\\\\\n&amp;\\leq2\\mathbb E\\sum_zd_z\n+\\frac2\\beta\\mathbb E\\sum_{z\\text{ marked}}|K_{j,z}|\\\\\n&amp;\\leq4\\Delta\\nu.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe same pointwise empirical argument with the hit-match fraction\n\\[\nf_{m,z}^{\\mathrm{hit}}\n=\\operatorname{avg}_{i\\in S}\\mathbf1_{E_i}r_i^{\\mathrm{path}}(z)\n\\]\ngives the stated conditional vertical estimate. Neither argument assumes independence of gradients and match indicators.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Endpoint conversion and ordered paths; original Section 7.3.** Under Lemmas 5.3–5.4, there are nonnegative exterior errors \\(e_{\\mathrm{law}}(\\mathcal Z)\\) such that every vertical law satisfies …", "statement": "<p>**Endpoint conversion and ordered paths; original Section 7.3.**</p><p>Under Lemmas 5.3–5.4, there are nonnegative exterior errors \\(e_{\\mathrm{law}}(\\mathcal Z)\\) such that every vertical law satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{r\\sim P_{\\mathrm{law}}}|F_{\\mathcal Z}(r)|\n\\leq4p_{\\mathrm{law}}v+e_{\\mathrm{law}}(\\mathcal Z),\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb Ee_{\\mathrm{law}}\\leq4\\Delta\\nu.\n\\]\nEvery ordered conjunction coupling satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal Z,r,r'}\n(F_{\\mathcal Z}(r)-F_{\\mathcal Z}(r'))_+\n\\leq20\\Delta\\nu,\n\\]\nand the iid conjunction and amplitude laws satisfy\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal Z,r,a}\n(|g_{\\mathcal Z}(r,a)|-F_{\\mathcal Z}(r))_+\n\\leq40\\Delta\\nu.\n\\]\nThese constants are independent of later stage sizes, cell counts, and descriptor counts.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choosing the adaptive descriptor \\(\\sigma\\), instead of an independent uniform descriptor, is dominated by \\(2^J\\): conditional on the remaining variables, uniform sampling selects it with probability \\(2^{-J}\\). The background and sign use none of the forced slot's fresh path data. Rebuilding the fine state in both cases gives, for each endpoint,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{avg}_{i\\in S}\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{forced}}\n\\frac{|h_j(B_{\\neg i}^j+sD^\\pm)\n-h_{j-1}(B_{\\neg i}^j+sD^\\pm)|}{s}\n\\leq\\Delta\\nu.\n\\]</p><p>For fixed path questions and data, the fine partition is fixed as \\(u\\) varies. Along\n\\[\nB(u)=B_{\\neg i}^j+s[(1-u)D^-+uD^+],\n\\]\ndifferentiation gives\n\\[\n\\frac1s\\frac d{du}h_j(B(u))\n=\\sum_zK_{j,z}(B(u))(D^+(z)-D^-(z)).\n\\]\nEvery slope is \\(\\sigma_{\\operatorname{par}(z)}w_z\\), with \\(0\\leq w_z\\leq2\\). Its negative part is bounded conservatively by \\(2\\sum_zd_z\\). Integrate in uniform \\(u\\), apply Lemma 5.4, and convert both endpoints. For every mode,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{avg}_{i\\in S}\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{forced}}\n\\left(\n\\frac{h_{j-1}(B_{\\neg i}^j+sD^-)\n-h_{j-1}(B_{\\neg i}^j+sD^+)}s\n\\right)_+\n\\leq10\\Delta\\nu.\n\\]</p><p>For a vertical mode, absolute slope is at most\n\\[\n2\\sum_z|K_{j,z}|r(z).\n\\]\nThe conditional hit estimate bounds its integral by \\(4p_{\\mathrm{law}}v\\), plus error of expectation at most \\(2\\Delta\\nu\\). Endpoint errors add at most \\(2\\Delta\\nu\\). Conditional Jensen gives the vertical assertion, including \\(p_{\\mathrm{law}}=0\\).</p><p>For \\(r\\leq r'\\), the difference of widths is the sum of the difference of upper endpoints and the reversed difference of lower endpoints. The modes\n\\[\n+\\sigma r\\to+\\sigma r',\n\\qquad-\\sigma r'\\to-\\sigma r\n\\]\nand subadditivity of positive parts give \\(20\\Delta\\nu\\).</p><p>For amplitude comparison, at one common-kernel realization write\n\\[\nH_a=\\varphi_{\\mathcal C}(B+sar),\\qquad\nH_-=\\varphi_{\\mathcal C}(B-s\\sigma r),\\qquad\nH_+=\\varphi_{\\mathcal C}(B+s\\sigma r).\n\\]\nFor independent \\(a'\\),\n\\[\n|H_a-H_{a'}|\n\\leq H_+-H_-\n+\\sum_{b\\in(a,a')}\n[(H_--H_b)_++(H_b-H_+)_+].\n\\]\nThe sum counts both amplitudes, including \\(a=a'\\). To verify it, expand \\(H_a-H_{a'}\\) through \\(H_+,H_-\\), bound exterior terms by positive parts, and repeat with \\(a,a'\\) exchanged.</p><p>Divide by \\(s\\), average over background and \\(a'\\), and use the common-kernel formulas. The absolute value of the averaged difference is \\(|g_{\\mathcal Z}(r,a)|\\), while the width averages to \\(F_{\\mathcal Z}(r)\\). The four error terms are supplied by the two amplitude modes, once for \\(a\\) and once for \\(a'\\). Applying the \\(10\\Delta\\nu\\) bound four times proves the last assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Selection of one exterior and threshold; original Section 7.4.** Under the low-cost hypothesis of Lemma 5.3, there exist an exterior value and a threshold \\( \\vartheta\\in[\\gamma/4,\\gamma/2] \\) for which …", "statement": "<p>**Selection of one exterior and threshold; original Section 7.4.**</p><p>Under the low-cost hypothesis of Lemma 5.3, there exist an exterior value and a threshold\n\\[\n\\vartheta\\in[\\gamma/4,\\gamma/2]\n\\]\nfor which\n\\[\nW(r)=\\mathbf1_{\\{F_{\\mathcal Z}(r)&gt;\\vartheta\\}}\n\\]\nis a function of the actual conjunction and satisfies the hypotheses of Proposition 4:\n\\[\n\\Pr_{P_*^t}[W=1]\\geq b_0vp,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Pr_{\\mathrm{pat}}[W=1]\\leq v(K_0p_{\\mathrm{pat}}+\\varepsilon),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Pr[W(r)&gt;W(r')]\\leq\\varepsilon v,\n\\qquad 0&lt;v\\leq B_0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(\\vartheta\\) uniformly and independently in \\([\\gamma/4,\\gamma/2]\\). Lemma 5.3 supplies mass at least \\(b_*p\\nu\\) with \\(|g_{\\mathcal Z}(r,a)|\\geq\\gamma\\) and \\(v\\leq B_*\\). If also \\(F_{\\mathcal Z}(r)\\leq\\gamma/2\\), the last positive part in Lemma 5.5 is at least \\(\\gamma/2\\), so this lost mass is at most \\(80\\Delta\\nu/\\gamma\\). The choice of \\(\\Delta\\) yields\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal Z,\\vartheta}\n\\left[\\mathbf1_{\\{v\\leq B_*\\}}\\Pr_{P_*^t}(W=1)\\right]\n\\geq b_*p\\nu/2.\n\\]</p><p>For each vertical law, \\(\\vartheta\\geq\\gamma/4\\) and Lemma 5.5 give\n\\[\n\\Pr_{\\mathrm{law}}(W=1)\n\\leq\\frac{16}{\\gamma}p_{\\mathrm{law}}v\n+\\frac4\\gamma e_{\\mathrm{law}}(\\mathcal Z).\n\\]\nThe final error has expectation at most \\(16\\Delta\\nu/\\gamma\\). For real \\(u,u'\\), threshold reversal probability is at most \\(4(u-u')_+/\\gamma\\). Thus every ordered law has\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal Z,\\vartheta}\n\\Pr[W(r)&gt;W(r')\\mid\\mathcal Z,\\vartheta]\n\\leq80\\Delta\\nu/\\gamma.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(e_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\) sum all \\(V\\) upper errors and all \\(Q\\) conditional violation probabilities. Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb Ee_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\leq A_t\\Delta\\nu/\\gamma.\n\\]\nDiscard exteriors with \\(e_{\\mathrm{tot}}&gt;\\varepsilon v\\). The iid vertical upper bound controls their integrated success by\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{16p}{\\gamma\\varepsilon}+1\\right)\n\\mathbb Ee_{\\mathrm{tot}}\n\\leq b_*p\\nu/4.\n\\]\nAt least \\(b_*p\\nu/4\\) integrated success remains with \\(v\\leq B_*\\) and \\(e_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\leq\\varepsilon v\\). Such an exterior with \\(v=0\\) has zero success. Since\n\\[\n\\mathbb Ev\\leq(T_0+1)\\nu,\\qquad b_0(T_0+1)=b_*/8,\n\\]\nsome remaining exterior with \\(v&gt;0\\) has success at least \\(b_0vp\\); otherwise integrated success would be at most \\(b_*p\\nu/8\\).</p><p>At that exterior, every error summand is at most \\(\\varepsilon v\\). With \\(K_0=16/\\gamma\\) and \\(B_0=B_*\\), all required inequalities follow. The kernel formula makes \\(W\\) depend only on the actual predicate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 7.1. Finite form structure", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For the demand and raw comparison measures constructed in Proposition 5:", "statement": "<p>For the demand and raw comparison measures constructed in Proposition 5:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Discrete seeds are polynomially enumerable, with rational probabilities of polynomial binary length and inverse-polynomial positive lower bounds.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. After finitely many fixed subdivisions of numerical boxes, conditional densities are nonnegative rational polynomials, and score-table entries are rational polynomials of bounded degree in a fixed number of numerical variables.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Only finitely many numerical polynomial patterns occur, independently of input size.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Total raw comparison mass is finite and bounded by a constant.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. For any fixed finite grid and deterministic entrywise rounding rule, every positive rounded demand mass or comparison-pair mass has an inverse-polynomial lower bound.</p>", "proof": "<p>Each primitive predicate has bounded arity and a polynomially enumerable presentation law. Conjunctions, paths, slots, stages, freeze subsets, descriptors, and resampling indicators use fixed numbers of samples once \\(T_0\\) is fixed. Enumerating their products therefore takes polynomial time. Probabilities are fixed-length products of rational primitive probabilities and fixed rational weights, preserving polynomial encoding and inverse-polynomial positive lower bounds.</p><p>Separate amplitude atoms at zero from their uniform parts. Remaining variables lie in rational intervals: amplitudes, interpolation times, noises, and derivative integration variables. Their joint densities are products of rational polynomial densities. Split absolute derivative weights into their two half intervals. No lookup-dependent descriptor defines a measure.</p><p>Fix a seed and an assignment to its bounded query list. Predicates become zero or one, descriptors become fixed signs, and marks are determined by finite counts. Score entries are sums of rational multiples of amplitudes, times, products of times and amplitudes, and noises. Local Boolean tables, label-collision patterns, descriptors, and mark sets range over fixed finite sets. Actual bit names only specify where a table is evaluated. Thus numerical polynomial patterns form a fixed finite family, with uniformly bounded arity and range.</p><p>Each differentiated-coordinate integral has mass at most \\(15/(8\\xi_j)\\), and there are finitely many formal cells and forms. Ordinary comparison measures have unit mass before their fixed external weights. Total mass is bounded.</p><p>For fixed rounding, each local rounded table or pair has mass equal to a fixed polynomial integral over a fixed region. There are finitely many patterns and rounded tables, so their positive conditional masses have a positive constant minimum. Any positive global rounded mass has a contributing positive seed. Its inverse-polynomial probability times this constant proves the last assertion; identification of equal functions only adds masses.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Finite compilation into exactly uniform demands; original Section 8.** Fix an integer \\(C'>1\\), and put \\( A_0=10^{12},\\qquad \\kappa=C'A_0,\\qquad T_0=100\\kappa. \\) Suppose the score system satisfies Propositions 5–6 …", "statement": "<p>**Finite compilation into exactly uniform demands; original Section 8.**</p><p>Fix an integer \\(C'&gt;1\\), and put\n\\[\nA_0=10^{12},\\qquad \\kappa=C'A_0,\\qquad T_0=100\\kappa.\n\\]\nSuppose the score system satisfies Propositions 5–6 for this \\(T_0\\).</p><p>One can construct in polynomial time a finite undirected graph with nonnegative rational capacities and unit demand on every distinct vertex pair, and set\n\\[\na=A_0/N^2,\n\\]\nso that:</p><p class=\"li\">A perfect witness gives a nonempty proper cut of ratio less than \\(a\\).</p><p class=\"li\">If every measurable lookup of positive demand variance satisfies \\(\\mathcal D(h)&gt;T_0\\operatorname{Var}_\\mu(h)\\), then every nonempty proper graph cut has ratio greater than \\(C'a\\).</p><p>All graph sizes and number encodings are polynomial in the input size, with constants and exponents depending only on \\(C'\\).</p><p>For canonical keys, let \\(b\\) be the number of proof-bit names. Unroll a complete demand or comparison experiment, including both endpoints and every primary, resampled, forced, path, and partition question list. Let \\(Q_*\\) bound its distinct queried names, and set \\(k=Q_*+1\\).</p><p>Choose a fixed rational grid in a bounded interval containing every score range. A key is a grid-valued function specified by its ordered essential bit names and truth table. Equal functions have the same key. Enumerate all keys of arity at most \\(k\\); with \\(g\\) grid values their number is at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^k\\binom bj g^{2^j}.\n\\]</p><p>Use nearest-grid-point rounding \\(R\\), with fixed tie rule and entrywise error at most \\(\\delta\\). If \\(W_0\\) is total raw comparison mass, choose\n\\[\n2\\delta W_0\\leq1,\\qquad \\delta&lt;1/16.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mu_i\\) be rounded demand masses and \\(c_{ij}^0\\) the raw comparison mass on unordered distinct key pairs, adding both orderings.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every experiment has at most \\(Q_*\\) bit names, so rounded scores belong to the enumerated key universe. The number of keys is polynomial because \\(k\\) and \\(g\\) are fixed. Every coloring extends to a measurable lookup by applying entrywise rounding and then its key color; define it arbitrarily on unused scores. The preimages are given by finitely many polynomial inequalities in numerical variables. Hence analytic soundness applies to every key coloring.</p><p>At every truth assignment,\n\\[\n|R(B)(x)-R(B')(x)|\n\\leq|B(x)-B'(x)|+2\\delta.\n\\]\nThus rounded truth cost is at most five, and the demand tails remain strictly outside \\([-1/8,1/8]\\). Also\n\\[\n\\mathcal D(h)=\\sum_{i&lt;j}c_{ij}^0|h_i-h_j|.\n\\]\nApply Lemmas 7.1–7.2 to form the graph and set \\(a=A_0/N^2\\).</p><p>For completeness, let \\(x\\) be a perfect witness, choose a common threshold \\(\\theta\\in[-1/8,1/8]\\), and color key \\(i\\) by\n\\[\nh_i=\\mathbf1_{\\{i(x)&gt;\\theta\\}}.\n\\]\nAll cluster copies have this color. Both sides have counting mass at least\n\\[\n\\tfrac12(10^{-4}-10^{-6})\\geq\\tau,\\qquad \\tau=10^{-4}/4.\n\\]\nFor uniform \\(\\theta\\), separation probability for values \\(u,v\\) is at most \\(4|u-v|\\). Expected raw rounded cut capacity is at most \\(4\\cdot5=20\\). Compilation excess adds at most one; total tether capacity is \\(2\\kappa\\alpha\\leq1/2\\); coherence edges do not cross. Some threshold cut has capacity at most \\(22\\). Its ratio is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{22}{N^2\\tau^2}&lt;\\frac{A_0}{N^2}=a,\n\\]\nbecause \\(A_0\\tau^2=625&gt;22\\).</p><p>For soundness, suppose a nonempty proper cut has capacity \\(T\\), counting mass \\(z\\), and ratio at most \\(C'a\\). Then\n\\[\nT\\leq\\kappa z(1-z)\\leq\\kappa/4.\n\\]\nA split cluster would cross a \\(2\\kappa\\) edge, so all clusters are monochromatic. Let \\(\\nu\\) be its rounded demand variance. Lemma 7.3 gives\n\\[\nT\\leq36\\kappa\\nu+\\kappa L_{\\mathrm{aux}}\n\\leq36\\kappa\\nu+T/2,\n\\]\nand hence \\(T\\leq72\\kappa\\nu\\). If \\(\\nu=0\\), these inequalities force \\(T=L_{\\mathrm{aux}}=0\\), and Lemma 7.3 gives \\(z(1-z)=0\\), contrary to a proper nonempty cut. Thus \\(\\nu&gt;0\\).</p><p>Since compiled capacities dominate raw capacities,\n\\[\n\\mathcal D(h)\\leq T\\leq72\\kappa\\nu&lt;T_0\\nu,\n\\]\ncontradicting analytic soundness. Every proper nonempty cut therefore has ratio greater than \\(C'a\\).</p><p>All enumerations, rational compilation, replication, and number encodings are polynomial for fixed \\(C'\\). The construction inspects neither a witness nor a candidate cut.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.1. Rational compilation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Proposition 7's setting, one can compute nonnegative rationals \\(c_{ij},l_i\\) in polynomial time with \\( c_{ij}\\geq c_{ij}^0,\\qquad \\sum_{i<j}(c_{ij}-c_{ij}^0)\\leq1, \\) …", "statement": "<p>In Proposition 7's setting, one can compute nonnegative rationals \\(c_{ij},l_i\\) in polynomial time with\n\\[\nc_{ij}\\geq c_{ij}^0,\\qquad\n\\sum_{i&lt;j}(c_{ij}-c_{ij}^0)\\leq1,\n\\]\n\\[\n0\\leq l_i\\leq\\mu_i,\\qquad\nL_{\\mathrm{ret}}:=\\sum_i l_i\\geq1-10^{-6}.\n\\]\nTheir binary lengths are polynomial, and every positive \\(l_i\\) is inverse-polynomially bounded below.</p><p>For\n\\[\n\\widehat\\mu_i=l_i/L_{\\mathrm{ret}},\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\widehat\\mu_i\\leq2\\mu_i,\\qquad\n\\widehat\\mu(E)\\geq\\mu(E)-10^{-6}\n\\]\nfor every set of keys \\(E\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Enumerate all discrete seeds. By Proposition 6, conditional numerical pieces have fixed-dimensional rational boxes, polynomial densities, and polynomial score entries from a fixed finite family.</p><p>Subdivide each box rationally. For each table entry and grid boundary, first check whether the polynomial is identically that boundary, and resolve such ties by the rule. Otherwise the equality set has measure zero: in one variable this is the finite-root bound; in higher dimensions expand in the last variable, apply induction to simultaneous coefficient vanishing, and integrate over fibers.</p><p>Coefficient bounds give rational Lipschitz constants. Comparing corner values with the Lipschitz bound times mesh identifies boxes with completely determined rounding. Apart from the null boundary sets, every point eventually lies in a resolved box. Bounded weights imply unresolved weighted mass tends to zero.</p><p>On a resolved box, integrate polynomial weights exactly term by term. For demand discard unresolved boxes. For capacities assign each unresolved box's full mass to every potentially occurring table pair. It suffices to use every grid-valued table on its at most \\(Q_*\\) queried names; their number is constant. This upper-bounds every pair mass, with excess bounded by that constant times unresolved mass.</p><p>Continue until total discarded demand is at most \\(10^{-6}\\) and total capacity excess at most one. The stopping tests are exact rational tests, weighting each unresolved conditional mass by its seed probability and counting all rectangular, formal-cell, and derivative-coordinate multiplicities. Explicitly, for conditional unresolved mass \\(U_{f,\\omega,\\ell}\\), seed weight \\(\\pi_{f,\\omega}\\), and candidate-pair bound \\(R_{f,\\omega,\\ell}\\), require\n\\[\n\\sum_f\\sum_\\omega\\pi_{f,\\omega}\n\\sum_\\ell R_{f,\\omega,\\ell}U_{f,\\omega,\\ell}\\leq1.\n\\]\nThe analogous demand sum omits the pair factor and is at most \\(10^{-6}\\).</p><p>A common mesh depth succeeds for the fixed finite pattern family; any earlier stopping depth is no larger. Positive retained conditional box masses, over these finitely many depths, have a positive constant rational minimum. A positive \\(l_i\\) includes one such mass times a positive seed probability, giving the inverse-polynomial bound. Polynomially many seeds and constant many boxes per seed give polynomial bit complexity.</p><p>Finally, \\(L_{\\mathrm{ret}}\\geq1-10^{-6}&gt;1/2\\) gives \\(\\widehat\\mu_i\\leq2\\mu_i\\). Also\n\\[\n\\widehat\\mu(E)\\geq l(E),\\qquad\n\\mu(E)-l(E)\\leq1-L_{\\mathrm{ret}}\\leq10^{-6}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Replication and tethers; original Section 8.2.** Let \\(m\\) be the number of canonical keys and \\(\\widehat\\mu\\) the rational probability law of Lemma 7.1.", "statement": "<p>**Replication and tethers; original Section 8.2.**</p><p>Let \\(m\\) be the number of canonical keys and \\(\\widehat\\mu\\) the rational probability law of Lemma 7.1. Choose integer \\(K\\) at least \\(4\\kappa m\\) and the reciprocal of the smallest positive \\(\\widehat\\mu_i\\).</p><p>Replace key \\(i\\) by a cluster of\n\\[\nn_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\lceil K\\widehat\\mu_i\\rceil,&amp;\\widehat\\mu_i&gt;0,\\\\\n1,&amp;\\widehat\\mu_i=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nChoose one representative per cluster, put \\(N=\\sum_i n_i\\), \\(w_i=n_i/N\\), and\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\sum_{\\widehat\\mu_i=0}w_i.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nK\\leq N\\leq K+m,\\qquad\n\\tfrac12\\widehat\\mu_i\\leq w_i\\leq2\\widehat\\mu_i\n\\quad(\\widehat\\mu_i&gt;0),\n\\qquad \\alpha\\leq1/(4\\kappa).\n\\]</p><p>Construct capacities by:</p><p class=\"li\">placing \\(c_{ij}\\) between representatives;</p><p class=\"li\">joining each nonrepresentative to its representative with capacity \\(2\\kappa\\);</p><p class=\"li\">for \\(\\widehat\\mu_i=0&lt;\\widehat\\mu_j\\), adding tether capacity\n\\[\n2\\kappa w_i\\widehat\\mu_j\n\\]\nbetween representatives.</p><p>Coincident capacities are added. All distinct vertex pairs have demand exactly one. This graph has polynomially many vertices and polynomial binary capacity encodings.</p>", "proof": "<p>The ceiling bounds give \\(K\\leq N\\leq K+m\\). Since \\(K\\geq m\\) and \\(K\\widehat\\mu_i\\geq1\\) for positive masses,\n\\[\nw_i\\geq\\frac{K\\widehat\\mu_i}{K+m}\\geq\\tfrac12\\widehat\\mu_i,\n\\qquad\nw_i\\leq\\frac{2K\\widehat\\mu_i}{K}=2\\widehat\\mu_i.\n\\]\nThere are at most \\(m\\) zero-mass singleton clusters, so\n\\[\n\\alpha\\leq m/K\\leq1/(4\\kappa).\n\\]\nLemma 7.1 permits polynomial-magnitude \\(K\\). The number of vertices, edges, and rational operations is polynomial, and all binary lengths remain polynomial.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "unnumbered claim, Equation (129). Counting mass versus demand variance", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a monochromatic-cluster coloring \\(h_i\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) of the graph in Lemma 7.2, let \\( z=\\sum_iw_ih_i,\\qquad \\nu=\\operatorname{Var}_\\mu(h), \\) and define …", "statement": "<p>For a monochromatic-cluster coloring \\(h_i\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) of the graph in Lemma 7.2, let\n\\[\nz=\\sum_iw_ih_i,\\qquad\n\\nu=\\operatorname{Var}_\\mu(h),\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\nL_{\\mathrm{aux}}\n=\\sum_{\\widehat\\mu_i=0}w_i\\sum_j\\widehat\\mu_j|h_i-h_j|.\n\\]\nThen the tethers contribute exactly \\(2\\kappa L_{\\mathrm{aux}}\\), and\n\\[\nz(1-z)\\leq36\\nu+L_{\\mathrm{aux}}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Draw a key with law \\(w\\). Retain it if its \\(\\widehat\\mu\\)-mass is positive; otherwise replace it by an independent \\(\\widehat\\mu\\) draw. The color changes with probability \\(L_{\\mathrm{aux}}\\). The replacement law is\n\\[\n\\rho_j=\\mathbf1_{\\{\\widehat\\mu_j&gt;0\\}}w_j+\\alpha\\widehat\\mu_j\n\\leq3\\widehat\\mu_j\\leq6\\mu_j.\n\\]\nApply this coupling independently to two \\(w\\) draws. Their original disagreement probability is \\(2z(1-z)\\), and replacement disagreement probability is at most\n\\[\n36\\cdot2\\nu\n\\]\nby \\(\\rho\\otimes\\rho\\leq36\\mu\\otimes\\mu\\). A union bound for the two color changes gives the assertion. The tether contribution is its defining capacity sum.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 5, "cite"], [1, 6, "cite"], [2, 3, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [3, 4, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [5, 0, "cite"], [5, 11, "cite"], [6, 9, "cite"], [6, 10, "cite"], [7, 5, "cite"], [8, 5, "cite"], [9, 5, "cite"], [10, 5, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"], [11, 19, "cite"], [11, 21, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 11, "cite"], [15, 0, "cite"], [15, 19, "cite"], [15, 21, "cite"], [15, 25, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [18, 15, "cite"], [19, 0, "cite"], [19, 27, "cite"], [20, 16, "cite"], [20, 17, "cite"], [20, 18, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [20, 22, "cite"], [21, 19, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [22, 19, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [22, 24, "cite"], [22, 25, "cite"], [23, 19, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [24, 19, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 19, "cite"], [26, 0, "cite"], [26, 19, "cite"], [26, 27, "cite"], [26, 28, "cite"], [27, 0, "cite"], [28, 27, "cite"], [28, 29, "cite"], [29, 27, "cite"], [29, 30, "cite"], [30, 27, "cite"]]});
